// THE ADDRESS ITSELF, ITS EQUALITY AND ITS CANONICAL SPELLING. The battery never holds a MAC as text: the
// candidate carries the value the extdeps codec parsed from the wire, the join compares values, and a refusal
// that has to name a candidate renders it back through the codec. That is the only way the two upstream
// spellings -- Redfish's uppercase colon-separated six octets, EDK2's lowercase packed twelve -- can be the
// same port, which is what the R5 join is for.
import extdeps.network.mac { Eui48Address, mac_addresses_are_equal, render_mac_address }
import gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_first_contact_standing { BmcFirmwareFamilyStanding, FamilyObserved, FamilyUnobserved }
import gunbc.machine_intake.bmc_firmware_family_discriminator {
  ManagerIdentityObservation, manager_identity_observation_of, discriminate_family, family_standing_from_discrimination,
}
import gunbc.bmc.supermicro_bmc_read {
  SupermicroVersionReading, SupermicroVirtualMediaOffer, cgi_version, supermicro_virtual_media_offer,
}

// THE FIRST-CONTACT BMC READ BATTERY.
//
// WHAT THIS IS. One read-only pass over access the operator has ALREADY given us, producing five readings per
// controller: (R1) firmware identity, (R2) capability bindings, (R3) the sensor-typing source, (R4) the console
// route, (R5) the boot NIC. Nothing here powers, boots, writes, or logs into anything: every reading is the
// decode of a body that a committed capture already holds, or of a body a dry world renders. The live transport
// that would take the reads that have never been taken is the live reader's, and it is declared here as a frontier with a
// named consumer and trigger — not implemented behind a fake.
//
// WHY THERE IS ONE BATTERY AND NOT TWO. gunbc.machine_intake.supermicro_bmc_reading already folded committed
// captures into identity, boot flags, SDR, SOL config and a virtual-media offer — for one family, with leaves
// that carried no standing and recorded a missing read as `none`/`[]`. A second, family-neutral battery beside
// it would fork the authority over what a BMC read means, so the acquisition root and the decode readers are
// generalized HERE and that module consumes them; its capture types are deleted in the same change rather than
// left as a second dialect.
//
// WHAT A READING IS NOT. A reading that was not obtained is a COVERAGE OBLIGATION in this observing layer,
// never a property of extdeps and never an absence in the world: `ReadingNotTaken { obligation }` names the read
// that would ground the value. It never reads as "the controller has no such thing" — that is the distinction
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.absent_reads_identically_to_never_looked draws — gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed, never conceal the
// deficit: a named refusal, never a silent empty — and the reason the carrier has three arms rather
// than an Option: `none` cannot tell "we looked and it said no" from "nobody ever looked".
//
// THE FOUR OUTCOMES, AND WHICH ONE A FAILURE IS (the rule the folds implement):
//   - the read is not in the source at all            -> ReadingNotTaken { obligation }
//   - the read is scheduled but its capture is missing -> ReadingRefused { cause } naming the file
//   - a capture's digest differs from the manifest     -> ReadingRefused { cause } naming the file and both digests
//   - the controller SERVED a 403/404/an empty-or-disabled resource -> ReadingEstablished with the negative
//     value: a controller that answers "no such resource" has answered, and recording that as a missing read
//     would lose the only evidence that the feature is absent rather than unexamined.
//   - a record whose controller or observed epoch is not the one asked for -> ReadingRefused: a reading copied
//     from another board, or from another moment, is not this board's reading.
//
// PROVENANCE IS PART OF THE READ, NOT A COMMENT BESIDE IT. `CommittedCapture` is a file in the tree with a
// manifest digest; `ModeledResponse` is a dry controller rendering its own state and carries NO hardware
// standing — a model that answers `mc info` says something about the model. `RuntimeRead` is the live reader's, and is not realized here. Only the
// zero-argument committed folds mint `ObservedByReceipt` (extdeps.external_authority's admit_callers gate); a
// fold handed an arbitrary source cannot mint a receipt for files it did not name.

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE ACQUISITION ROOT — generalized from supermicro_bmc_reading's SupermicroCapture and CaptureFold, which
// this replaces.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE OPERATION, IN ITS UPSTREAM'S TERMS. A Redfish GET is identified by its resource and an ipmitool
// invocation by its subcommand: those are the two dialects the captures exist in, and a read identity that
// invented a third would be a key nothing can check a manifest against.
// HOW THE READ WAS ASKED FOR, in the upstream's own terms: a Redfish GET, an ipmitool invocation, or a request
// on a vendor's own HTTP surface (Supermicro's /cgi/ipmi.cgi pages, MegaRAC's own endpoints). The third arm
// exists so a family's private endpoint is still recorded as A REQUEST with a method and a target rather than
// smuggled into the ipmitool arm; the battery reads no semantics from it, and no reading depends on it.
type BmcReadOperation
  = RedfishGet { uri: NonEmptyStr }
  | IpmiToolCommand { command: NonEmptyStr }
  | VendorHttpRequest { method: NonEmptyStr, target: NonEmptyStr }

// WHAT THE TRANSPORT RECORDED ABOUT THE ATTEMPT, or nothing. `OutcomeNotRecorded` is honest for captures whose
// transport did not write a status down; it is NOT a synonym for success, and no reading may be established
// from an outcome it cannot name (the folds ask for the outcome where one matters).
type BmcReadOutcome
  = HttpStatusRecorded { code: Int }
  | CommandExitRecorded { code: Int }
  | OutcomeNotRecorded

// WHERE THE BYTES CAME FROM. Read this arm before trusting any standing beside it.
type BmcReadProvenance
  = CommittedCapture { manifest: NonEmptyStr }
  | ModeledResponse { realization: NonEmptyStr }
  | RuntimeRead { session: NonEmptyStr }

// ONE READ, CARRIED AS A RECORD. `read` is the read identity (one of the `bmc_*_read` names below);
// `controller` is which BMC the read is ABOUT (its MAC text, or the endpoint that answered) and `observed_at`
// is when; the two are what make a body attributable, and the fold refuses a record that does not match the
// board and the epoch it was asked for rather than quietly folding in a neighbour's answer.
type BmcReadRecord {
  controller: NonEmptyStr
  read: NonEmptyStr
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr
  operation: BmcReadOperation
  outcome: BmcReadOutcome
  digest: NonEmptyStr
  body: String
  provenance: BmcReadProvenance
}

// THE MANIFEST ROW: what a committed capture set DECLARES before any file is opened — path and the digest the
// capture was taken with. The digest is checked against the bytes read, never assumed.
type BmcCaptureManifestRow {
  controller: NonEmptyStr
  read: NonEmptyStr
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr
  operation: BmcReadOperation
  outcome: BmcReadOutcome
  path: NonEmptyStr
  digest: NonEmptyStr
  // WHICH OBSERVATION OF A BUNDLE. Some captures were written by the intake script as one JSON bundle holding
  // several command outputs under `observations`; a row that names a key is read as that observation, and the
  // digest still pins the WHOLE bundle. A row that names none is read as the file's own bytes — the ordinary
  // case, and the one where the capture really is one response.
  intake_key: NonEmptyStr?
}

// A DRY CONTROLLER'S ANSWER, rendered from world state by the realization that owns it
// (gunbc.bmc_dry_realization). There is no digest to check against an external manifest, so the digest is the
// rendering's own — and `provenance` says `ModeledResponse`, which is what stops a model answer being spent as
// hardware standing.
type BmcModeledResponse {
  read: NonEmptyStr
  operation: BmcReadOperation
  outcome: BmcReadOutcome
  body: String
}

// THE SOURCE: one interface, two realizations here and a third in the live reader. Parsing always happens in the extdeps
// wire readers; no structure crosses this boundary unparsed.
type BmcReadSource
  = CommittedCaptureSet { controller: NonEmptyStr, manifest: List<BmcCaptureManifestRow> }
  | ModeledController { controller: NonEmptyStr, observed_at: NonEmptyStr, realization: NonEmptyStr, responses: List<BmcModeledResponse> }

// WHAT A SOURCE ANSWERS FOR ONE REQUESTED READ. Note the two failure arms are different facts: a scheduled
// capture that cannot be fetched is a REFUSAL (something was promised and broke), while a read that is not in
// the source at all is a coverage obligation (nobody ever promised it) — collapsing them is how a missing
// capture starts reporting as "the controller does not have that".
type BmcReadAttempt
  = ReadRecorded { record: BmcReadRecord }
  | ReadRefusedToFetch { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | ReadNotScheduled { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// THE READS THE BATTERY TAKES, by name. These are the read identities the manifests are written against; a
// manifest row naming something else is a mistake that shows up as ReadNotScheduled, not as a silent hole.
data bmc_mc_info_read: NonEmptyStr = "ipmitool mc info"
data bmc_redfish_manager_read: NonEmptyStr = "GET /redfish/v1/Managers/<id>"
data bmc_supermicro_cgi_generic_info_read: NonEmptyStr = "POST /cgi/ipmi.cgi GENERIC_INFO"
data bmc_ipmi_sdr_elist_read: NonEmptyStr = "ipmitool sdr elist"
data bmc_redfish_thermal_read: NonEmptyStr = "GET /redfish/v1/Chassis/<id>/Thermal"
data bmc_ipmi_sol_info_read: NonEmptyStr = "ipmitool sol info <channel>"
data bmc_redfish_boot_options_read: NonEmptyStr = "GET /redfish/v1/Systems/<id>/BootOptions"
data bmc_redfish_ethernet_interface_read: NonEmptyStr = "GET /redfish/v1/Systems/<id>/EthernetInterfaces/<member>"
data bmc_redfish_ethernet_interfaces_collection_read: NonEmptyStr = "GET /redfish/v1/Systems/<id>/EthernetInterfaces"
data bmc_redfish_sensors_read: NonEmptyStr = "GET /redfish/v1/Chassis/<id>/Sensors"
data bmc_redfish_serial_interfaces_read: NonEmptyStr = "GET /redfish/v1/Managers/<id>/SerialInterfaces"

// THE DEBTS, SPELLED ONCE, for the arm where every source was attempted and none of the other arms applied. An
// assembled obligation is fine when there is something to assemble; an EMPTY one would tell a reader "nothing is
// owed here" about a reading that has nothing established -- the deficit would be concealed rather than
// recorded. Each reading names its own unread sources instead, and the arms that DO assemble from per-read
// obligations are unchanged.
data bmc_firmware_identity_obligation: NonEmptyStr =
  "no firmware-identity source (Redfish Managers, IPMI Get Device ID via mc info, Supermicro CGI genericinfo) has been read for this controller"

data bmc_sensor_typing_obligation: NonEmptyStr =
  "no sensor-typing source (ipmitool sdr elist, Redfish Thermal, Redfish Sensors) has been read for this controller"

data bmc_console_route_obligation: NonEmptyStr =
  "no console-route read (ipmitool sol info, Redfish SerialInterfaces) has been taken for this controller"
data bmc_redfish_virtual_media_read: NonEmptyStr = "GET /redfish/v1/Managers/<id>/VirtualMedia/<member>"

// The ComputerSystem resource: its `Boot` object states the boot SOURCE the firmware was told to use. It is
// consumed only to report what the controller itself says about boot selection — it names no interface and no
// MAC, so it never yields a boot NIC and mints no capability (DESIGN).
data bmc_redfish_systems_read: NonEmptyStr = "GET /redfish/v1/Systems/<id>"

// THE DECLARED FRONTIER. Each read the battery asks for carries the obligation that is owed when it has never
// been taken — who needs it and what would take it. This is the live reader's contract in data form: the runtime reader
// has a named consumer (this battery) and a named trigger (a controller whose reading reports these
// obligations), and until it exists the readings stay NotTaken rather than becoming zeroes.
type BmcReadRequest { read: NonEmptyStr, obligation: NonEmptyStr }

data bmc_first_contact_read_plan: List<BmcReadRequest> = [
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_mc_info_read,
    obligation: "the family-neutral Get Device ID report has never been taken for this controller; the live reader frontier takes `ipmitool mc info` on first contact" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_redfish_manager_read,
    obligation: "the Redfish manager resource has never been read for this controller; the live reader frontier reads Managers/<id> for FirmwareVersion and the OEM keys the family gate consumes" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_ipmi_sdr_elist_read,
    obligation: "the SDR repository has never been listed for this controller; the live reader frontier takes `ipmitool sdr elist` to establish what types the sensors" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_redfish_thermal_read,
    obligation: "the Redfish thermal telemetry surface has never been read for this controller; the live reader frontier reads Chassis/<id>/Thermal" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_ipmi_sol_info_read,
    obligation: "the SOL configuration has never been read for this controller; the live reader frontier takes `ipmitool sol info <channel>`" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_redfish_boot_options_read,
    obligation: "the boot option collection has never been read for this controller; the live reader frontier reads Systems/<id>/BootOptions" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_redfish_systems_read,
    obligation: "the ComputerSystem resource has never been read for this controller; the live reader frontier reads Systems/<id> for the Boot object whose override state R5 reports alongside the NIC question" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_redfish_ethernet_interface_read,
    obligation: "the EthernetInterface resources have never been read for this controller; the live reader frontier reads Systems/<id>/EthernetInterfaces and each member" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_redfish_sensors_read,
    obligation: "the Redfish sensor collection has never been read for this controller; the live reader frontier reads Chassis/<id>/Sensors" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_redfish_serial_interfaces_read,
    obligation: "the Redfish serial-interface service has never been read for this controller; the live reader frontier reads Managers/<id>/SerialInterfaces" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_redfish_virtual_media_read,
    obligation: "the virtual-media service has never been probed for this controller; the live reader frontier reads Managers/<id>/VirtualMedia/<member> — the only admissible evidence that virtual media exists on a board" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  BmcReadRequest {
    read: bmc_supermicro_cgi_generic_info_read,
    obligation: "the Supermicro generic-info page has never been read for this controller; the live reader frontier posts /cgi/ipmi.cgi GENERIC_INFO" as NonEmptyStr,
  },
]

// THE STANDING POLICY, SUPPLIED BY THE CALLER. Standing is not derived from bytes alone: a committed capture
// read from the tree by a fold that NAMED the file is what may carry an observation receipt, and a fold handed
// an arbitrary source must be handed the standing rather than minting one. A modeled controller can never
// carry hardware standing, so the caller that renders a world passes TranscribedUncited with the read
// obligation that still lies ahead of it.
type BmcReadingStandings {
  committed: CitedFigureStanding
  modeled: CitedFigureStanding
  runtime: CitedFigureStanding
}

fn bmc_read_standing_for(provenance: BmcReadProvenance, standings: BmcReadingStandings) -> CitedFigureStanding {
  match provenance {
    CommittedCapture { manifest: m } => standings.committed
    ModeledResponse { realization: r } => standings.modeled
    RuntimeRead { session: s } => standings.runtime
  }
}

// THE PROBE'S OWN FRAMING, STRIPPED IN THE OBSERVING LAYER. Some captures in this tree were written by the
// operator's own probe script, which appended the transport's verdict as a final `HTTP_CODE:<n>` line after the
// service's bytes. That line belongs to the probe, not to the controller: it is read here, becomes the record's
// OUTCOME, and is removed before any extdeps wire reader sees the body — a Redfish body with a status line glued
// on is not a Manager document, and refusing it would blame the controller for its recorder's format. A body
// with no such line is passed through untouched and gets no status: nothing here invents one.
type BmcProbeFraming { body: String, status: Int? }

fn bmc_probe_framing(text: String) -> BmcProbeFraming {
  let lines = filter(split(s: text, delimiter: "\n"), line => trim(s: line) != "")
  match list_at_optional(xs: lines, index: count(lines) - 1) {
    Absent => BmcProbeFraming { body: text, status: none }
    Present { value: last } => match bmc_probe_status(text: trim(s: last)) {
      Present { value: status } => BmcProbeFraming { body: join(bmc_all_but_last(lines: lines), "\n"), status: Present { value: status } }
      Absent => BmcProbeFraming { body: text, status: none }
    }
  }
}

// THE MARKER, OR NOTHING. A line that begins with the marker but does not carry a number is left in the body:
// the framing is a convention, and text this layer cannot account for is passed to the wire readers rather than
// quietly deleted.
fn bmc_probe_status(text: String) -> Int? {
  if starts_with(s: text, prefix: "HTTP_CODE:") == false {
    none
  } else {
    match list_at_optional(xs: split(s: text, delimiter: ":"), index: 1) {
      Present { value: digits } => parse_int(s: trim(s: digits))
      Absent => none
    }
  }
}

fn bmc_all_but_last(lines: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  let head: List<String> = []
  fold(lines, init: head, f: (acc, line) =>
    if count(acc) < count(lines) - 1 { concat(acc, [line]) } else { acc }
  )
}

// ONE FILE OF A COMMITTED MANIFEST, read and digest-checked. Three named refusals, never an empty body: the
// file would not open, or it is not the bytes the manifest promised. A digest mismatch REFUSES the reading
// (witness: the same fold over a capture whose bytes were edited reports ReadingRefused, not an established
// value computed from the edited text).
fn bmc_read_committed_row(row: BmcCaptureManifestRow) -> BmcReadAttempt uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match bmc_read_row(row: row) {
    RowRecorded { record: record } => ReadRecorded { record: record }
    RowFailed { failure: failure } => ReadRefusedToFetch {
      cause: concat("the capture ", failure.path as String, " was refused: ", failure.cause) as NonEmptyStr
    }
  }
}

// THE ONE LOOKUP, over either realization. A requested read that no row answers is `ReadNotScheduled` carrying
// the plan's obligation text; a row that exists but cannot be fetched or is not this board's is a refusal.
fn bmc_read_of(source: BmcReadSource, request: BmcReadRequest) -> BmcReadAttempt uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match source {
    CommittedCaptureSet { controller: controller, manifest: manifest } =>
      match bmc_manifest_row_for(manifest: manifest, controller: controller, read: request.read) {
        Absent => ReadNotScheduled { obligation: request.obligation }
        Present { value: row } => bmc_read_committed_row(row: row)
      }
    ModeledController { controller: controller, observed_at: epoch, realization: realization, responses: responses } =>
      match bmc_modeled_response_for(responses: responses, read: request.read) {
        Absent => ReadNotScheduled { obligation: request.obligation }
        Present { value: response } => ReadRecorded { record: BmcReadRecord {
          controller: controller,
          read: response.read,
          observed_at: epoch,
          operation: response.operation,
          outcome: response.outcome,
          digest: sha256_hex_of_text(text: response.body),
          body: response.body,
          provenance: ModeledResponse { realization: realization },
        } }
      }
  }
}

fn bmc_manifest_row_for(manifest: List<BmcCaptureManifestRow>, controller: NonEmptyStr, read: NonEmptyStr) -> BmcCaptureManifestRow? {
  let rows = filter(manifest, row => row.read == read && row.controller == controller)
  match rows.first() {
    Present { value: row } => Present { value: row }
    Absent => none
  }
}

fn bmc_modeled_response_for(responses: List<BmcModeledResponse>, read: NonEmptyStr) -> BmcModeledResponse? {
  let rows = filter(responses, response => response.read == read)
  match rows.first() {
    Present { value: response } => Present { value: response }
    Absent => none
  }
}

// THE ATTRIBUTION GATE. A record is usable only if it is about the controller being read and was taken at the
// epoch the fold was asked about. Wrong-board and wrong-epoch are REDs with positive controls in the witness:
// the same body filed under another controller refuses, and filed under the right one establishes.
fn bmc_record_is_for(record: BmcReadRecord, controller: NonEmptyStr, observed_at: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  record.controller == controller && record.observed_at == observed_at
}

fn bmc_usable_record(
  source: BmcReadSource,
  request: BmcReadRequest,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
) -> BmcReadAttempt uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match bmc_read_of(source: source, request: request) {
    ReadRecorded { record: record } =>
      if bmc_record_is_for(record: record, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at) {
        ReadRecorded { record: record }
      } else {
        ReadRefusedToFetch {
          cause: concat(
            "the record for ", request.read as String, " is filed under controller ", record.controller as String,
            " observed ", record.observed_at as String, ", not this board at ", observed_at as String,
          ) as NonEmptyStr
        }
      }
    ReadRefusedToFetch { cause: cause } => ReadRefusedToFetch { cause: cause }
    ReadNotScheduled { obligation: obligation } => ReadNotScheduled { obligation: obligation }
  }
}

// THE BODY, OR WHY THERE IS NONE. Every reading starts here, so the three arms below map one-to-one onto the
// three arms of the carrier — a reading cannot invent a value that this function did not hand it.
type BmcBodyAttempt
  = BodyAvailable { body: String, standing: CitedFigureStanding, provenance: BmcReadProvenance }
  | BodyRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | BodyOwed { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

fn bmc_body_of(
  source: BmcReadSource,
  request: BmcReadRequest,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
  standings: BmcReadingStandings,
) -> BmcBodyAttempt uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match bmc_usable_record(source: source, request: request, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at) {
    ReadRecorded { record: record } => BodyAvailable {
      body: record.body,
      standing: bmc_read_standing_for(provenance: record.provenance, standings: standings),
      provenance: record.provenance,
    }
    ReadRefusedToFetch { cause: cause } => BodyRefused { cause: cause }
    ReadNotScheduled { obligation: obligation } => BodyOwed { obligation: obligation }
  }
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE CARRIER.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ONE READING, THREE ARMS. `ReadingEstablished` carries the value WITH the standing that licenses it — a
// value and its evidence travel together or not at all (gunbc.design_argument::single_authority: extdeps decides what the wire says, this
// layer decides what may be believed of it). `ReadingRefused` is a read that was attempted and came back a
// typed refusal. `ReadingNotTaken` is the coverage obligation, and it never reads as absence.
type FirstContactReading<T>
  = ReadingEstablished { value: T, standing: CitedFigureStanding }
  | ReadingRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | ReadingNotTaken { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

fn reading_refused<T>(cause: NonEmptyStr) -> FirstContactReading<T> {
  ReadingRefused { cause: cause }
}

fn reading_not_taken<T>(obligation: NonEmptyStr) -> FirstContactReading<T> {
  ReadingNotTaken { obligation: obligation }
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// R1 — FIRMWARE IDENTITY: ONE READING PER SOURCE, RECONCILED ON THE DERIVED IDENTITY.
//
// The three sources that can answer for a board are kept APART until they are compared, and the comparison is
// on the release identity each upstream's own reader derived — never on a normalized version string. That is
// the generalization of supermicro_bmc_read.supermicro_identity_agrees_with_catalog: agreement is a fact about
// what the sources derived, and disagreement REFUSES rather than picking a winner. A fold that normalized
// `1.52` and `01.52.25` into the same key would report agreement for reasons that have nothing to do with the
// board, which is the failure this section exists to make unreachable.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE SOURCES, in the dialects they answer in. `mc info` is the FAMILY-NEUTRAL one: every family answers Get
// Device ID, so it is the identity source for a board whose management service is absent, refused, or not yet
// written a reader for.
type FirmwareIdentitySourceName
  = McInfoSource
  | RedfishManagerSource
  | SupermicroCgiGenericInfoSource

// WHAT ONE SOURCE SAID, with the standing that source's read carries. `source` is the read identity, so every
// evidence row traces back to a manifest row (or to a dry controller's rendering) rather than floating free.
// `device_available` is kept only where the source is `mc info`, because only that report claims the controller
// is ready to answer: an identity taken while the controller says it is mid-initialization is not an identity
// to store.
// WHAT ONE SOURCE SAYS ABOUT THE RELEASE, and how strongly it says it.
//
// `identified` and `components` are NOT two spellings of one fact, and keeping them side by side without saying
// so is what let a two-component source masquerade as an exact release. They state different things:
//   - `components` is what the source actually measured -- `mc info` publishes a major and a minor and nothing
//     else, so its list has two entries and states no patch.
//   - `identified` is a release identity: the release the source named, spelled as that source spelled it.
// WHETHER THIS SOURCE COULD STATE THE WHOLE RELEASE is a fact about that source's WIRE FORMAT, declared by the
// producer that builds the evidence, beside the reason -- and `bmc_exact_evidence` / `bmc_partial_evidence`
// partition on that field and nothing else. It is NOT computed from the company a source happens to keep: a
// width over the set would make the same `mc info` answer exact on a board whose other sources are silent and
// partial beside a Managers resource, would promote a lone two-component answer to the release a capability row
// is bound against, and would leave the reason nobody checked.
type FirmwareIdentityEvidence {
  source: NonEmptyStr
  named: FirmwareIdentitySourceName
  identified: BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity
  components: List<BmcVersionComponent>
  states_full_release: Bool
  standing: CitedFigureStanding
  device_available: Bool?
}

// THE NUMBERS A SOURCE ACTUALLY NAMED. `ipmitool mc info` reports the firmware revision as two integers; a
// Redfish manager prints a vendor string that the upstream's own parser reads into three. Comparing those two
// claims as STRINGS would either refuse every board that has both sources (ipmitool's `1.52` is not the text
// `01.52.25`) or quietly normalize one into the other — and normalizing is how `01.52.25` and `1.52.25` become
// "the same firmware" for reasons nobody checked. So the comparison is on the components each source named,
// taken from the upstream's own parse: two sources concur when every component BOTH of them names is equal, and
// a component only one of them names says nothing either way.
type BmcVersionComponent { part: NonEmptyStr, value: Int }

fn bmc_component(part: String, value: Int) -> BmcVersionComponent {
  BmcVersionComponent { part: part as NonEmptyStr, value: value }
}

fn bmc_semantic_components(version: FirmwareSemanticVersion) -> List<BmcVersionComponent> {
  [
    bmc_component(part: "major", value: version.major),
    bmc_component(part: "minor", value: version.minor),
    bmc_component(part: "patch", value: version.patch),
  ]
}

fn bmc_components_concur(a: List<BmcVersionComponent>, b: List<BmcVersionComponent>) -> Bool {
  count(filter(a, left => bmc_component_conflicts(left: left, others: b))) == 0
}

// THE EXACT OBSERVATIONS: the sources whose wire format states a whole release, deduplicated by the identity
// they named. Two sources naming ONE release collapse to one exact identity; two sources naming two DIFFERENT
// full releases must never both sit here, and `bmc_exact_evidence_refusal` is what stops R2 from choosing
// between them by list order.
fn bmc_exact_evidence(evidences: List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence>) -> List<ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence> {
  let some = map(filter(evidences, evidence => evidence.states_full_release), evidence => ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence { evidence: evidence })
  let empty: List<ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence> = []
  fold(some, init: empty, f: (acc, exact) => if bmc_exact_identity_collected(exact: exact, others: acc) { acc } else { concat(acc, [exact]) })
}

// A REPEAT OF AN IDENTITY ALREADY COLLECTED. Two sources naming one release contribute one candidate, not two.
fn bmc_exact_identity_collected(exact: ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence, others: List<ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence>) -> Bool {
  count(filter(others, other => bmc_firmware_release_identity_eq(a: other.evidence.identified, b: exact.evidence.identified))) > 0
}

// THE PARTIAL OBSERVATIONS: the sources that stated fewer components than the widest. They corroborate or
// conflict; they never become the release a capability row is bound against.
fn bmc_partial_evidence(evidences: List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence>) -> List<PartialFirmwareIdentityEvidence> {
  map(filter(evidences, evidence => evidence.states_full_release == false), evidence => PartialFirmwareIdentityEvidence { evidence: evidence })
}

// DOES A PARTIAL OBSERVATION FIT THE EXACT RELEASE? Only components both sources state are compared: a source
// that never stated a patch neither contradicts a patch nor confirms it.
fn bmc_evidence_concur(partial: FirmwareIdentityEvidence, exact: FirmwareIdentityEvidence) -> Bool {
  bmc_components_concur(a: partial.components, b: exact.components)
}

fn bmc_component_conflicts(left: BmcVersionComponent, others: List<BmcVersionComponent>) -> Bool {
  count(filter(others, right => right.part == left.part && right.value != left.value)) > 0
}

// THE R1 VALUE: the release the sources ESTABLISHED, the partial observations that corroborate it, what
// refused, what is owed. All four are carried so a consumer can tell "one source, corroborated by nobody" from
// "three sources, all agreeing" without re-running the fold, and so a failed source is visible instead of
// silently shrinking the list.
//
// `exact` holds AT MOST ONE element, and that is the whole point of the split: R2 binds capability rows by exact
// release identity, so the release it binds against must come from a source that stated one, and there must not
// be two candidates for it. `agreed` is the corroborating set -- the exact evidence plus every partial that
// shares its components -- and it is what a consumer reads to see how many sources spoke.
type FirmwareIdentityReading {
  exact: List<ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence>
  partials: List<PartialFirmwareIdentityEvidence>
  agreed: List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence>
  refused: List<NonEmptyStr>
  owed: List<NonEmptyStr>
}

// AN EXACT OBSERVATION: the source stated a whole release identity.
type ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence { evidence: FirmwareIdentityEvidence }

// A PARTIAL OBSERVATION: the source stated some components and no release. It may corroborate an exact
// observation and it may conflict with one; it can never be the release a capability row is bound against.
type PartialFirmwareIdentityEvidence { evidence: FirmwareIdentityEvidence }

// NO EXACT RELEASE, AND WHY. Two exact identities is a refusal of its own, not a shorter agreed list: the
// alternative is letting the order of a list decide which release R2 binds against.
type FirmwareIdentityReadingRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// NOTHING ESTABLISHED ANYTHING: the coverage obligation, spelled from what was refused and what was never read.
type FirmwareIdentityReadingOwed { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// `mc info` → a release identity, spelled as ipmitool spells it. The identity is the report's Firmware Revision
// through the upstream's own rendering, so the comparison key is the tool's output and not a re-derivation.
//
// THIS SOURCE IS PARTIAL. `mc info` publishes a major and a minor and nothing else, so the component list has
// two entries, and `bmc_firmware_release_identity_of_wire` yields no `semantic_version` for a two-part wire
// text: the evidence lands in `partials` and never becomes the release R2 binds against. That is not a defect
// of the read -- it is what the read states, and the type says so.
fn bmc_mc_info_evidence(
  read: NonEmptyStr,
  report: IpmiMcInfoReport,
  family: BmcFirmwareFamily,
  standing: CitedFigureStanding,
) -> FirmwareIdentityEvidence {
  FirmwareIdentityEvidence {
    source: read,
    named: McInfoSource,
    identified: bmc_firmware_release_identity_of_wire(family: family, wire: ipmitool_mc_info_firmware_text(report: report)),
    components: [
      bmc_component(part: "major", value: report.identity.firmware_revision_major),
      bmc_component(part: "minor", value: report.identity.firmware_revision_minor),
    ],
    // The IPMI Device ID response has no field for a patch: its firmware-revision bytes carry a major and an
    // auxiliary revision, so this source cannot state a three-part release however the numbers line up.
    states_full_release: false,
    standing: standing,
    device_available: Present { value: report.device_available },
  }
}

// THE RECONCILIATION. Any two sources that derived different release identities refuse the whole reading,
// naming both sources and both spellings so the operator knows which read to re-take. With no disagreement and
// at least one source the reading is established, carrying the WEAKEST standing among the sources that agreed:
// a hardware capture corroborated by a dry rendering is only as good as the rendering, because a rendering
// says nothing about hardware. With no source at all, the reading is the coverage obligation.
type FirmwareIdentityDisagreement { left: FirmwareIdentityEvidence, right: FirmwareIdentityEvidence }

fn bmc_first_disagreement(evidences: List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence>) -> FirmwareIdentityDisagreement? {
  match evidences.first() {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: head } => match bmc_disagreeing_with(head: head, others: evidences) {
      Absent => none
      Present { value: other } => Present { value: FirmwareIdentityDisagreement { left: head, right: other } }
    }
  }
}

fn bmc_disagreeing_with(head: FirmwareIdentityEvidence, others: List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence>) -> FirmwareIdentityEvidence? {
  let differing = filter(others, other => bmc_evidence_disagrees(a: head, b: other))
  match differing.first() {
    Present { value: other } => Present { value: other }
    Absent => none
  }
}

// DO THESE TWO OBSERVATIONS CONTRADICT EACH OTHER? Components in common, different values: yes. Two exact
// observations naming different releases: yes, even when their component lists happen to agree -- the component
// list is a lossy view of the release, and the release is what R2 binds against.
//
// Two observations from the SAME source are not a contradiction between sources, and this refuses them anyway:
// a source that yields two different readings of one controller is a broken acquisition, and the honest outcome
// is a refusal naming it. That is reachable -- a manifest that lists the same read twice, or a family with two
// readers of one name -- and the fold never sees it today only because the committed manifests do not repeat a
// read.
fn bmc_evidence_disagrees(a: FirmwareIdentityEvidence, b: FirmwareIdentityEvidence) -> Bool {
  if bmc_components_concur(a: a.components, b: b.components) == false {
    true
  } else {
    if a.named == b.named && bmc_firmware_release_identity_eq(a: a.identified, b: b.identified) == false {
      true
    } else {
      if a.source == b.source && bmc_firmware_release_identity_eq(a: a.identified, b: b.identified) == false {
        true
      } else {
        false
      }
    }
  }
}

// WHAT THE SOURCES ADD UP TO, and what this function actually enforces, in the order it enforces it:
//   1. any two observations that contradict each other (different values for a component both state, or two
//      different release identities, or two different readings from one source) REFUSE the reading, naming both
//      sources and both spellings;
//   2. more than one distinct EXACT release identity REFUSES the reading -- never a silent pick, because R2
//      binds capability rows by exact identity and a list order must not decide which one it uses;
//   3. with one exact release, every partial must fit it (a partial that contradicts it was refused at 1);
//   4. with no exact release but at least one partial, the reading is established on the partials and R2 is
//      owed an obligation naming the read that would state a release;
//   5. with nothing established, the reading is a refusal if anything was attempted and refused, and the
//      coverage obligation if nothing was.
// The standing carried is the WEAKEST among the sources that spoke: a hardware capture corroborated by a dry
// rendering is only as good as the rendering, because a rendering says nothing about hardware.
fn bmc_firmware_identity_of(
  evidences: List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence>,
  refused: List<NonEmptyStr>,
  owed: List<NonEmptyStr>,
) -> FirstContactReading<FirmwareIdentityReading> {
  let exact = bmc_exact_evidence(evidences: evidences)
  let partials = bmc_partial_evidence(evidences: evidences)
  match bmc_first_disagreement(evidences: evidences) {
    Present { value: pair } => reading_refused(concat(
      "the firmware sources disagree: ", pair.left.source as String, " says ",
      pair.left.identified.raw_version as String, " while ", pair.right.source as String, " says ",
      pair.right.identified.raw_version as String,
    ) as NonEmptyStr)
    Absent => match bmc_exact_evidence_refusal(exact: exact) {
      Present { value: cause } => reading_refused(cause)
      Absent => match bmc_exact_evidence_of(exact: exact) {
        Present { value: winner } => ReadingEstablished {
          value: FirmwareIdentityReading {
            exact: [winner],
            partials: partials,
            agreed: bmc_agreeing_evidence(evidences: evidences, winner: winner),
            refused: refused,
            owed: bmc_partial_only_owed(partials: partials, owed: owed),
          },
          standing: bmc_evidences_standing(evidences: evidences),
        }
        Absent =>
          if count(partials) > 0 {
            ReadingEstablished {
              value: FirmwareIdentityReading { exact: [], partials: partials, agreed: evidences, refused: refused, owed: bmc_partial_only_owed(partials: partials, owed: owed) },
              standing: bmc_evidences_standing(evidences: evidences),
            }
          } else {
            if count(refused) > 0 {
              reading_refused(join(map(refused, cause => cause as String), "; ") as NonEmptyStr)
            } else {
              if count(owed) > 0 {
                reading_not_taken(join(map(owed, obligation => obligation as String), "; ") as NonEmptyStr)
              } else {
                reading_not_taken(bmc_firmware_identity_obligation)
              }
            }
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE ONE EXACT OBSERVATION, when there is exactly one.
fn bmc_exact_evidence_of(exact: List<ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence>) -> ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence? {
  if count(exact) == 1 {
    match exact.first() {
      Present { value: winner } => Present { value: winner }
      Absent => none
    }
  } else {
    none
  }
}

// TWO DISTINCT EXACT IDENTITIES: refuse, and name them. This is the case the component comparison used to wave
// through (1.52 and 01.52.25 share their major and minor), and it is the case in which a list order would
// otherwise pick the release R2 binds against. `bmc_exact_evidence` already collapsed repeats of ONE identity,
// so what is left here is exactly the distinct candidates -- counting them rather than re-deriving a distinctness
// rule keeps one identity comparison in the module.
fn bmc_exact_evidence_refusal(exact: List<ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence>) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  if count(exact) > 1 {
    Present { value: concat("the sources state more than one exact release identity: ", bmc_exact_identity_texts(exact: exact)) as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    none
  }
}

fn bmc_exact_identity_texts(exact: List<ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence>) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(map(exact, item => concat(item.evidence.source as String, " says ", item.evidence.identified.raw_version as String)), "; ") as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE CORROBORATING SET, in the order the sources answered: every source that spoke and does not conflict with
// the bound release. That includes a SECOND source stating the same release in full -- two sources naming one
// release is corroboration, and dropping the second would record one row where two agree, which is the mirror
// image of promoting a partial -- plus every partial whose shared components match. A source that contradicts
// never reaches here: step 1 refused the reading. The filters below are safety nets, not the mechanism that
// keeps conflicts out.
fn bmc_agreeing_evidence(evidences: List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence>, winner: ExactFirmwareIdentityEvidence) -> List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence> {
  filter(evidences, evidence =>
    if evidence.states_full_release {
      bmc_firmware_release_identity_eq(a: evidence.identified, b: winner.evidence.identified)
    } else {
      bmc_evidence_concur(partial: evidence, exact: winner.evidence)
    })
}

// A PARTIAL-ONLY ESTABLISHMENT OWES THE READ THAT WOULD STATE A RELEASE. Spelled once, because a fold that
// typed its own version of this sentence could quietly owe less.
fn bmc_partial_only_owed(partials: List<PartialFirmwareIdentityEvidence>, owed: List<NonEmptyStr>) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  if count(partials) > 0 {
    concat(owed, [bmc_exact_release_obligation])
  } else {
    owed
  }
}

data bmc_exact_release_obligation: NonEmptyStr =
  "no source stated a full release identity: the sources that spoke published firmware components only, and a capability row cannot be bound against a partial observation -- the read that would state the release is owed" as NonEmptyStr

// THE STANDING LATTICE, ordered by what a standing licenses: an observation receipt licenses a statement about
// hardware, a citation licenses a transcription of an upstream document, and `TranscribedUncited` licenses
// neither — it is the arm that says nobody has read the thing yet. The weakest standing is the minimum rank.
fn bmc_standing_rank(standing: CitedFigureStanding) -> Int {
  match standing {
    ObservedByReceipt { observed: observed } => 3
    CitedToAuthority { authority: authority } => 2
    TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: obligation } => 1
  }
}

fn bmc_weaker_standing(a: CitedFigureStanding, b: CitedFigureStanding) -> CitedFigureStanding {
  if bmc_standing_rank(standing: a) <= bmc_standing_rank(standing: b) { a } else { b }
}

// The identity is as well-grounded as its WORST agreeing source — but it starts from a source that WAS read,
// not from a floor that would flatten an ObservedByReceipt into a transcription. Folding from an obligation
// constant would make a committed capture indistinguishable from a typed-up note.
fn bmc_evidences_standing(evidences: List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence>) -> CitedFigureStanding {
  match evidences.first() {
    Present { value: first } =>
      fold(
        evidences,
        init: first.standing,
        f: (acc, evidence) => bmc_weaker_standing(a: acc, b: evidence.standing),
      )
    Absent => TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: "no source has been read for this controller" as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

// THE THREE R1 SOURCES, FOLDED. Each source's read is requested independently; a source whose read is owed
// lands in `owed`, one whose body cannot be fetched or whose reader refuses lands in `refused`, and a source
// that answered lands in `agreed` — subject to the reconciliation above, which is where a disagreement becomes
// visible instead of becoming a value.
//
// ONE BODY, ONE CLASSIFICATION. Each source's body is parsed ONCE and lands in exactly one arm. These used to be
// three functions per source (evidence / refusal / obligation) that each re-parsed the same body and re-matched
// the same arms: three chances for one body's classification to disagree with itself, and three parses of the
// same bytes. The arm is decided where the body is read.
type BmcSourceAttempt
  = SourceAnswered { evidence: FirmwareIdentityEvidence }
  | SourceRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | SourceOwed { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// One body → evidence, refusal, or obligation. `mc info` is the interesting case: a report that decodes but says
// `Device Available : no` is kept (the claim is part of the evidence) rather than discarded, because the report
// is what the controller said and the consumer decides what to do with a mid-initialization answer.
fn bmc_mc_info_body_attempt(body: BmcBodyAttempt, family: BmcFirmwareFamily) -> BmcSourceAttempt {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match ipmitool_mc_info_of(stdout: text) {
        McInfoReported { report: report } => SourceAnswered { evidence: bmc_mc_info_evidence(read: bmc_mc_info_read, report: report, family: family, standing: standing) }
        McInfoUnreadable { cause: cause } => SourceRefused { cause: concat("mc info: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => SourceRefused { cause: concat("mc info: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => SourceOwed { obligation: obligation }
  }
}

fn bmc_manager_body_attempt(body: BmcBodyAttempt, family: BmcFirmwareFamily) -> BmcSourceAttempt {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match manager_identity_observation_of(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: observation } =>
          if observation.firmware_version == "" {
            SourceRefused { cause: "Redfish Manager: the manager resource publishes no FirmwareVersion" as NonEmptyStr }
          } else {
            SourceAnswered { evidence: FirmwareIdentityEvidence {
              source: bmc_redfish_manager_read,
              named: RedfishManagerSource,
              identified: bmc_firmware_release_identity_of_wire(family: family, wire: observation.firmware_version as NonEmptyStr),
              components: bmc_identity_components(wire: observation.firmware_version),
              // The Managers resource publishes the release the service ships, in the service's own spelling:
              // whatever its shape, that is a whole release, so this source can be the one R2 binds a
              // capability row against.
              states_full_release: true,
              standing: standing,
              device_available: none,
            } }
          }
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => SourceRefused { cause: concat("Redfish Manager: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => SourceRefused { cause: concat("Redfish Manager: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => SourceOwed { obligation: obligation }
  }
}

// The Supermicro CGI arm. `genericinfo` prints its own firmware version, which the Supermicro reader parses into
// a semantic version; the release identity is then spelled by that reader's own renderer. This is the one source
// whose raw wire spelling the upstream reader does not retain, so the comparison key is its rendering — and
// that is why a CGI-only board is never R1-established against a second source it does not have.
fn bmc_cgi_body_attempt(body: BmcBodyAttempt, family: BmcFirmwareFamily) -> BmcSourceAttempt {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match cgi_version(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: reading } => SourceAnswered { evidence: FirmwareIdentityEvidence {
          source: bmc_supermicro_cgi_generic_info_read,
          named: SupermicroCgiGenericInfoSource,
          identified: BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity {
            family: family,
            raw_version: firmware_semantic_version_text(version: reading.version) as NonEmptyStr,
            semantic_version: Present { value: reading.version },
          },
          components: bmc_semantic_components(version: reading.version),
          // The CGI prints its full firmware version string and the upstream reader parses it into a semantic
          // version, so this source states a whole release too.
          states_full_release: true,
          standing: standing,
          device_available: none,
        } }
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => SourceRefused { cause: concat("Supermicro CGI genericinfo: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => SourceRefused { cause: concat("Supermicro CGI genericinfo: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => SourceOwed { obligation: obligation }
  }
}

// THE TALLY. The arms of the three sources collected in one pass: what answered, what refused and why, and what
// was never read.
type BmcSourceTally {
  evidences: List<FirmwareIdentityEvidence>
  refused: List<NonEmptyStr>
  owed: List<NonEmptyStr>
}

fn bmc_tally_of(attempts: List<BmcSourceAttempt>) -> BmcSourceTally {
  fold(
    attempts,
    init: BmcSourceTally { evidences: [], refused: [], owed: [] },
    f: (acc, attempt) => match attempt {
      SourceAnswered { evidence: evidence } => BmcSourceTally { evidences: concat(acc.evidences, [evidence]), refused: acc.refused, owed: acc.owed }
      SourceRefused { cause: cause } => BmcSourceTally { evidences: acc.evidences, refused: concat(acc.refused, [cause]), owed: acc.owed }
      SourceOwed { obligation: obligation } => BmcSourceTally { evidences: acc.evidences, refused: acc.refused, owed: concat(acc.owed, [obligation]) }
    },
  )
}

// THE READING OVER THE THREE SOURCES: ask each source for its read, classify each body once, reconcile what
// answered. The three bodies are requested independently so one missing capture never suppresses another
// source's answer; the reconciliation in `bmc_firmware_identity_of` is what turns two answers into one identity,
// or into a refusal that names both.
fn bmc_firmware_identity_reading(
  source: BmcReadSource,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
  family: BmcFirmwareFamily,
  standings: BmcReadingStandings,
) -> FirstContactReading<FirmwareIdentityReading> uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let mc = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_mc_info_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  let manager = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_redfish_manager_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  let cgi = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_supermicro_cgi_generic_info_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  let tally = bmc_tally_of(attempts: [
    bmc_mc_info_body_attempt(body: mc, family: family),
    bmc_manager_body_attempt(body: manager, family: family),
    bmc_cgi_body_attempt(body: cgi, family: family),
  ])
  bmc_firmware_identity_of(evidences: tally.evidences, refused: tally.refused, owed: tally.owed)
}


// THE COMPONENTS A SOURCE NAMES. Each source spells its version in its own arity — ipmitool's `mc info` gives
// major.minor, a Redfish manager gives major.minor.patch, the Supermicro CGI page gives all three — so agreement
// is decided component by component over what each source actually NAMED, never by normalizing one dialect into
// another's spelling. A component a source does not name can never conflict; a component both name must match.
fn bmc_identity_components(wire: NonEmptyStr) -> List<BmcVersionComponent> {
  let parts = split(s: wire as String, delimiter: ".")
  concat(
    concat(bmc_named_component(parts: parts, at: 0, name: "major"), bmc_named_component(parts: parts, at: 1, name: "minor")),
    bmc_named_component(parts: parts, at: 2, name: "patch"),
  )
}

fn bmc_named_component(parts: List<String>, at: Int, name: String) -> List<BmcVersionComponent> {
  match list_at_optional(xs: parts, index: at) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: text } => match parse_int(s: trim(s: text)) {
      Absent => []
      Present { value: number } => [bmc_component(part: name, value: number)]
    }
  }
}







// THE PLAN LOOKUP. A read the battery asks for that is not in the plan has no obligation text, which would make
// its NotTaken arm an empty string — so the read names and their obligations live in one table and are looked
// up rather than invented at the call site.
fn bmc_request_for(read: NonEmptyStr) -> BmcReadRequest {
  let rows = filter(bmc_first_contact_read_plan, row => row.read == read)
  match rows.first() {
    Present { value: row } => row
    Absent => BmcReadRequest { read: read, obligation: concat("the read ", read as String, " has never been taken for this controller") as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

fn bmc_filter_some(values: List<NonEmptyStr?>) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  let kept: List<NonEmptyStr> = []
  fold(values, init: kept, f: (acc, value) => match value {
    Present { value: v } => concat(acc, [v])
    Absent => acc
  })
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// R2 — CAPABILITY BINDINGS, PER CAPABILITY, EACH WITH ITS OWN EVIDENCE.
//
// There is deliberately NO outer standing on R2 and no `capabilities: List<BmcCapability>` field. A list of
// capabilities with one standing beside it is how a board ends up "supporting" virtual media because its
// release is in a catalogue that was cited for a different capability. Each capability therefore carries the
// basis on which THIS board is believed to have it: a catalogue row, a surface the controller itself offered
// in a read, or an operation with an observation receipt behind it. A capability with none of those is not in
// the list — and is not recorded as absent either, unless a read actually said it is not served.
//
// TWO THINGS R2 CANNOT MINT (both operator rulings, both REDs with positive controls in the witness):
//   - VIRTUAL MEDIA is never inferred from a host-side NIC, from `cdc_ether`, or from any indirect sign. The
//     only admissible evidence is a read of the controller's own virtual-media service (or a catalogue row for
//     the release, or an exercised operation). A dry world whose host NIC inventory is full of gadget NICs and
//     whose VirtualMedia read was never taken establishes nothing.
//   - PXE IS NOT A BMC CAPABILITY, and neither is a boot NIC. An established R5 boot NIC, or a PXE line on a
//     console, mints no BmcCapability of any kind: R2's bases are catalogue rows and management-service reads,
//     and a network fact about the host is neither.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type CapabilityEvidenceBasis
  = Catalogued { catalogued_in: NonEmptyStr }
  | SurfaceOffered { read: NonEmptyStr }
  | OperationExercised { receipt: ObservedReceipt }

// A CAPABILITY FACT AND THE EVIDENCE THAT ESTABLISHES IT, as one value. The standing travels with the pair.
//
// It cannot sit on the list R2 returns, and it cannot be borrowed from R1. Three bases, three different
// strengths: a capability whose basis is a catalogue row is established by the CATALOGUE FACT (its standing
// comes from the binding, which the catalogue's owner supplies); a capability whose basis is a surface the
// controller itself published is established by THAT SURFACE READ (its standing is that read's standing); a
// capability whose basis is an operation actually exercised is established by that operation's receipt. One
// standing over a list that mixes them would let a hardware-observed firmware identity lend its
// ObservedByReceipt to a capability whose only basis is a hand-written catalogue row -- the overclaim the
// accepted v2 note removed, and the reason the standing is a field of the pair and not of the reading.
type BmcCapabilityEvidence {
  capability: BmcCapability
  basis: CapabilityEvidenceBasis
  standing: CitedFigureStanding
}

// THE ONE WAY AN EXERCISED OPERATION BECOMES EVIDENCE: the basis and the standing are read off the same receipt,
// so they cannot disagree. No realization in this PR supplies this basis -- the live reader (the declared
// frontier `bmc_read_plan` names it) is its consumer; it is spelled here because the alternative is a caller
// pairing a receipt with whatever standing it liked.
fn bmc_exercised_capability_evidence(capability: BmcCapability, receipt: ObservedReceipt) -> BmcCapabilityEvidence {
  BmcCapabilityEvidence {
    capability: capability,
    basis: OperationExercised { receipt: receipt },
    standing: ObservedByReceipt { observed: receipt },
  }
}

// R2'S OWN CARRIER, rather than FirstContactReading<List<BmcCapabilityEvidence>>. The other four readings rest
// on one acquisition each, so one standing describes the whole reading; R2 does not, and the accepted repair is
// a list of individually-standing facts rather than one standing over a heterogeneous list. The three arms are
// the same three states the other readings use -- established, refused, owed -- spelled out here because the
// established arm has nowhere honest to put a standing.
type CapabilityReading
  = CapabilitiesEstablished { evidence: List<BmcCapabilityEvidence> }
  | CapabilitiesRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | CapabilitiesOwed { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// THE CATALOGUE BINDING, supplied by the consumer that owns the catalogue (MegaRAC rows come from
// gunbc.host.bmc_release_capability_catalog, ASRock rows from extdeps.boards.asrock_rack). It is handed in
// rather than looked up here so that the battery's closure does not decide which board's catalogue is
// authoritative, and so a release that is simply NOT in any catalogue says so as an obligation. The binding
// carries the CATALOGUE FACT'S OWN STANDING for the same reason: the battery does not decide how well-cited
// somebody else's catalogue is, and it must not upgrade it by association with a hardware read.
type BmcCapabilityBinding
  = BindingCatalogued { rows: List<BmcFirmwareReleaseCapabilityRow>, catalogued_in: NonEmptyStr, standing: CitedFigureStanding }
  | BindingAbsent { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// WHAT A CONTROLLER WITH NO CATALOGUE ROW OWES. Stated once, because every committed fold that has no row for
// the release it reads owes exactly this obligation; a fold that typed its own version could quietly owe less.
data bmc_capability_binding_absent_obligation: NonEmptyStr =
  "no capability row names this release: the catalogue carries no row for the firmware this controller runs, and the battery mints no capability of its own" as NonEmptyStr

fn bmc_capability_evidence_of(
  from_release: FirstContactReading<FirmwareIdentityReading>,
  binding: BmcCapabilityBinding,
  source: BmcReadSource,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
  standings: BmcReadingStandings,
) -> CapabilityReading uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match from_release {
    ReadingRefused { cause: cause } => CapabilitiesOwed { obligation: concat("capabilities: no established release identity to bind a capability row against (", concat(cause as String, "); no catalogue row names this release until an identity is agreed")) as NonEmptyStr }
    ReadingNotTaken { obligation: obligation } => CapabilitiesOwed { obligation: concat("capabilities: ", obligation as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    ReadingEstablished { value: reading, standing: standing } =>
      match bmc_established_release(reading: reading) {
        // NOTHING EXACT TO BIND AGAINST. The sources published components only, so the read R2 owes is the one
        // that states a release -- naming a catalogue that has no row for a release NOBODY STATED would point
        // the reader at the wrong read.
        Absent => CapabilitiesOwed { obligation: concat("capabilities: ", bmc_exact_release_obligation) as NonEmptyStr }
        Present { value: identified } => match bmc_release_row_for(rows: bmc_binding_rows(binding: binding), wanted: Present { value: identified }) {
          Absent =>
            match binding {
              BindingAbsent { obligation: obligation } => CapabilitiesOwed { obligation: obligation }
              BindingCatalogued { rows: rows, catalogued_in: catalogue, standing: catalogue_standing } => CapabilitiesOwed { obligation: bmc_uncatalogued_release_obligation(catalogue: catalogue, reading: reading) }
            }
          // NO STANDING ON THE READING: each element of the list carries its own, from its own basis. The
          // release identity is what the row was looked up with; it is not evidence for any capability.
          Present { value: row } => CapabilitiesEstablished { evidence: concat(
            bmc_catalogued_evidence_of(row: row, binding: binding),
            bmc_offered_evidence_of(source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings),
          ) }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn bmc_binding_rows(binding: BmcCapabilityBinding) -> List<BmcFirmwareReleaseCapabilityRow> {
  match binding {
    BindingCatalogued { rows: rows, catalogued_in: catalogue, standing: standing } => rows
    BindingAbsent { obligation: obligation } => []
  }
}

// THE STANDING OF THE CATALOGUE FACT, as the catalogue's owner supplied it. A binding that is absent has no
// catalogue fact to stand on, and no capability may be established on it.
fn bmc_binding_standing(binding: BmcCapabilityBinding) -> CitedFigureStanding? {
  match binding {
    BindingCatalogued { rows: rows, catalogued_in: catalogue, standing: standing } => Present { value: standing }
    BindingAbsent { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

// THE RELEASE R2 BINDS AGAINST: the one exact identity R1 established. It is not "the first evidence" -- an
// agreed set holds at most one exact identity by construction (bmc_firmware_identity_of refuses on two), and
// partials are not candidates because they do not state a release.
fn bmc_established_release(reading: FirmwareIdentityReading) -> BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity? {
  match reading.exact.first() {
    // The wrapper carries the observation; the observation carries the identity it established.
    Present { value: wrapper } => Present { value: wrapper.evidence.identified }
    Absent => none
  }
}

// THE RELEASE IS ESTABLISHED AND NO ROW IN THE SUPPLIED CATALOGUE NAMES IT: an obligation naming the release,
// not a capability. Spelled once so a fold cannot type its own version of the sentence and owe less.
fn bmc_uncatalogued_release_obligation(catalogue: NonEmptyStr, reading: FirmwareIdentityReading) -> NonEmptyStr {
  concat("no capability row in ", concat(catalogue as String, concat(" names release ", concat(bmc_established_release_text(reading: reading), "; the release is uncatalogued")))) as NonEmptyStr
}

fn bmc_established_release_text(reading: FirmwareIdentityReading) -> String {
  match bmc_established_release(reading: reading) {
    Present { value: identified } => identified.raw_version as String
    Absent => "(no established release)"
  }
}

fn bmc_release_row_for(rows: List<BmcFirmwareReleaseCapabilityRow>, wanted: BmcFirmwareReleaseIdentity?) -> BmcFirmwareReleaseCapabilityRow? {
  match wanted {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: identity } => {
      let hits = filter(rows, row => bmc_firmware_release_identity_eq(a: row.firmware, b: identity))
      match hits.first() {
        Present { value: row } => Present { value: row }
        Absent => none
      }
    }
  }
}

// EVERY CAPABILITY IN THE ROW, each with the catalogue as its own basis. The row's `catalogued_in` is kept
// because "which catalogue, and where is its citation" is the question a reviewer asks of a capability list.
fn bmc_catalogued_evidence_of(row: BmcFirmwareReleaseCapabilityRow, binding: BmcCapabilityBinding) -> List<BmcCapabilityEvidence> {
  let catalogue = match binding {
    BindingCatalogued { rows: rows, catalogued_in: catalogued_in, standing: standing } => catalogued_in
    BindingAbsent { obligation: obligation } => "an unknown catalogue" as NonEmptyStr
  }
  match bmc_binding_standing(binding: binding) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: carried } => map(row.capabilities, capability => BmcCapabilityEvidence {
      capability: capability,
      basis: Catalogued { catalogued_in: catalogue },
      standing: carried,
    })
  }
}

// THE SURFACES THE CONTROLLER ITSELF OFFERED. Two reads can offer a surface: the virtual-media service (the
// only admissible route to CapabilityVirtualMedia on a board that is not in a catalogue) and the SOL
// configuration (Enabled: yes is the controller advertising its own serial-over-LAN service). Anything else a
// caller believes it can infer is not consulted here — that is the operator ruling, written as code.
fn bmc_offered_evidence_of(
  source: BmcReadSource,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
  standings: BmcReadingStandings,
) -> List<BmcCapabilityEvidence> uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let virtual_media = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_redfish_virtual_media_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  let sol = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_ipmi_sol_info_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  bmc_capability_some([
    bmc_virtual_media_surface(body: virtual_media),
    bmc_sol_surface(body: sol),
  ])
}

fn bmc_virtual_media_surface(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> BmcCapabilityEvidence? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match supermicro_virtual_media_offer(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: offer } => Present { value: BmcCapabilityEvidence {
          capability: CapabilityVirtualMedia,
          basis: SurfaceOffered { read: bmc_redfish_virtual_media_read },
          standing: standing,
        } }
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => none
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

fn bmc_sol_surface(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> BmcCapabilityEvidence? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match ipmi_sol_configuration_of_sol_info(stdout: text) {
        Present { value: configuration } =>
          if configuration.enabled {
            Present { value: BmcCapabilityEvidence {
              capability: CapabilitySerialConsole,
              basis: SurfaceOffered { read: bmc_ipmi_sol_info_read },
              standing: standing,
            } }
          } else {
            none
          }
        Absent => none
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

fn bmc_capability_some(values: List<BmcCapabilityEvidence?>) -> List<BmcCapabilityEvidence> {
  let kept: List<BmcCapabilityEvidence> = []
  fold(values, init: kept, f: (acc, value) => match value {
    Present { value: v } => concat(acc, [v])
    Absent => acc
  })
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// R3 — WHICH SOURCE TYPES THE SENSORS, AND OVER WHAT.
//
// No type in the tree said "which source is typing this board's sensors", so this is the one new sum the
// battery adds (v1's gap table named it). It is a LIST, one observation per read, and there is no selection:
// a board that has both an SDR repository and a Redfish thermal surface reports both, because choosing one
// silently is how a board ends up monitored through the surface that answers least. Each observation carries
// the population it covers and the rows it read UNINTERPRETED — a decoded line is decoded, a line that did not
// decode is carried as refusal text rather than dropped, so a truncated listing cannot masquerade as a board
// with no sensors — the empty-observation narrow gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.absent_reads_identically_to_never_looked
// names).
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type SensorTypingObservation
  = IpmiSdrTyping {
      population: NonEmptyStr,
      decoded: List<SdrTypeLine>,
      unstructured: List<String>,
    }
  | RedfishThermalTyping {
      surface: RedfishThermalTelemetrySurface,
      population: NonEmptyStr,
      members: List<NonEmptyStr>,
    }

fn bmc_sensor_typing_reading(
  source: BmcReadSource,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
  standings: BmcReadingStandings,
) -> FirstContactReading<List<SensorTypingObservation>> uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let sdr = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_ipmi_sdr_elist_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  let thermal = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_redfish_thermal_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  let sensors = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_redfish_sensors_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  let observations = bmc_typing_some([
    bmc_sdr_typing_of(body: sdr),
    bmc_thermal_typing_of(body: thermal, surface: RedfishLegacyChassisThermal),
    bmc_sensors_typing_of(body: sensors),
  ])
  let refused = bmc_filter_some([
    bmc_sdr_typing_refusal(body: sdr),
    bmc_thermal_typing_refusal(body: thermal, surface: RedfishLegacyChassisThermal),
    bmc_sensors_typing_refusal(body: sensors),
  ])
  let owed = bmc_filter_some([
    bmc_typing_owed(body: sdr),
    bmc_typing_owed(body: thermal),
    bmc_typing_owed(body: sensors),
  ])
  let standing = bmc_typing_standing(bodies: [sdr, thermal, sensors], observations: observations)
  if count(observations) > 0 {
    ReadingEstablished { value: observations, standing: standing }
  } else {
    if count(refused) > 0 {
      reading_refused(join(map(refused, cause => cause as String), "; ") as NonEmptyStr)
    } else {
      // A NAMED DEBT, never an empty string. With the arms above an empty `owed` list is not reachable -- every
      // body that arrived has produced an observation or a refusal -- but a reading whose obligation text were
      // "" would conceal the deficit it exists to record, so the fallback is spelled once, as data.
      if count(owed) > 0 {
        reading_not_taken(join(map(owed, obligation => obligation as String), "; ") as NonEmptyStr)
      } else {
        reading_not_taken(bmc_sensor_typing_obligation)
      }
    }
  }
}

// A listing that produced NOTHING — no decoded line and no unstructured line — is a refusal, not an empty
// typing source. An empty body and a body of noise are different facts from "the controller has no sensors",
// and only the third may be reported as a sensor inventory of zero.
fn bmc_sdr_typing_of(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> SensorTypingObservation? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } => {
      let lines = sdr_decode_lines(stdout: text)
      let decoded = bmc_decoded_sdr_lines(lines: lines)
      let unstructured = bmc_unstructured_sdr_lines(lines: lines)
      if count(decoded) == 0 && count(unstructured) == 0 {
        none
      } else {
        Present { value: IpmiSdrTyping {
          population: concat("the SDR repository, ", int_to_decimal_string(value: count(lines)), " line(s) listed") as NonEmptyStr,
          decoded: decoded,
          unstructured: unstructured,
        } }
      }
    }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

fn bmc_decoded_sdr_lines(lines: List<SdrLineResult>) -> List<SdrTypeLine> {
  let empty: List<SdrTypeLine> = []
  fold(lines, init: empty, f: (acc, line) => match line {
    SdrLineDecoded { line: decoded } => concat(acc, [decoded])
    SdrLineUnstructured { text: text } => acc
  })
}

fn bmc_unstructured_sdr_lines(lines: List<SdrLineResult>) -> List<String> {
  let empty: List<String> = []
  fold(lines, init: empty, f: (acc, line) => match line {
    SdrLineDecoded { line: decoded } => acc
    SdrLineUnstructured { text: text } => concat(acc, [text])
  })
}

fn bmc_sdr_typing_refusal(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      if count(sdr_decode_lines(stdout: text)) == 0 {
        Present { value: "the SDR listing contained no lines at all" as NonEmptyStr }
      } else {
        none
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => Present { value: concat("SDR listing: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

fn bmc_thermal_typing_of(body: BmcBodyAttempt, surface: RedfishThermalTelemetrySurface) -> SensorTypingObservation? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match redfish_member_ids(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: ids } =>
          if count(ids) == 0 {
            // The service SERVED a Members array and it is EMPTY. That is an answer about the surface, not a gap
            // in the read, and it is recorded as one: the alternative is a reading that reports an obligation for
            // a source it has already read -- the narrow failure mode this reading exists to avoid, "absent reads
            // identically to never looked". The population text says which of the two it was.
            Present { value: RedfishThermalTyping { surface: surface, population: "the chassis thermal collection, which publishes no members" as NonEmptyStr, members: [] } }
          } else {
            Present { value: RedfishThermalTyping { surface: surface, population: "the chassis thermal collection" as NonEmptyStr, members: ids } }
          }
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => bmc_thermal_arrays_typing_of(text: text, surface: surface)
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

// No `Members` array: this is a Thermal RESOURCE, which types its sensors in inline `Temperatures`/`Fans`
// arrays. A resource that names nothing is no typing source (the caller keeps the obligation); a resource that
// names eleven temperatures is one, and is never reported as an inventory of zero.
fn bmc_thermal_arrays_typing_of(text: String, surface: RedfishThermalTelemetrySurface) -> SensorTypingObservation? {
  match redfish_thermal_sensors_of(body: text) {
    ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => none
    ObservationEstablished { observed: listing } =>
      if count(listing.temperatures) + count(listing.fans) == 0 {
        none
      } else {
        Present { value: RedfishThermalTyping {
          surface: surface,
          population: concat(
            concat(concat("the chassis thermal resource: ", int_to_decimal_string(value: count(listing.temperatures))), " temperature(s), "),
            concat(int_to_decimal_string(value: count(listing.fans)), " fan(s) named by the resource"),
          ) as NonEmptyStr,
          members: concat(listing.temperatures, listing.fans),
        } }
      }
  }
}

fn bmc_sensors_typing_of(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> SensorTypingObservation? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match redfish_member_ids(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: ids } =>
          if count(ids) == 0 {
            // The same distinction as in `bmc_thermal_typing_of`: an empty Members array is what the service
            // SAID, so it is an observation over an empty population, never the obligation of an unread source.
            Present { value: RedfishThermalTyping { surface: RedfishThermalSubsystemSensorCollection, population: "the Redfish sensor collection, which publishes no members" as NonEmptyStr, members: [] } }
          } else {
            Present { value: RedfishThermalTyping { surface: RedfishThermalSubsystemSensorCollection, population: "the Redfish sensor collection" as NonEmptyStr, members: ids } }
          }
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => none
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

fn bmc_thermal_typing_refusal(body: BmcBodyAttempt, surface: RedfishThermalTelemetrySurface) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match redfish_member_ids(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: ids } => none
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => Present { value: concat("Redfish telemetry: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => Present { value: concat("Redfish telemetry: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

fn bmc_sensors_typing_refusal(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match redfish_member_ids(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: ids } => none
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => Present { value: concat("Redfish sensors: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => Present { value: concat("Redfish sensors: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

fn bmc_typing_owed(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } => none
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => Present { value: obligation }
  }
}

fn bmc_typing_some(values: List<SensorTypingObservation?>) -> List<SensorTypingObservation> {
  let kept: List<SensorTypingObservation> = []
  fold(values, init: kept, f: (acc, value) => match value {
    Present { value: v } => concat(acc, [v])
    Absent => acc
  })
}

fn bmc_typing_standing(bodies: List<BmcBodyAttempt>, observations: List<SensorTypingObservation>) -> CitedFigureStanding {
  if count(observations) == 0 {
    TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: "no sensor-typing source has been read for this controller" as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    fold(
      bodies,
      init: TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: "no sensor-typing source has been read for this controller" as NonEmptyStr },
      f: (acc, body) => match body {
        BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } => bmc_weaker_standing(a: acc, b: standing)
        BodyRefused { cause: cause } => acc
        BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => acc
      },
    )
  }
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// R4 — THE CONSOLE ROUTE, AND WHAT KIND OF CONSOLE EVIDENCE IT IS.
//
// Three kinds, and the difference between them is the whole reading. `ConsoleConfigured` is a CONFIGURATION
// read — `ipmitool sol info` answered `Enabled: true` — which licenses saying the controller is set up to
// expose a serial-over-LAN channel. It does NOT license saying a console worked, and a reading that was taken
// this way can never be re-labelled a session. `ConsoleServiceAdvertised` is the Redfish service publishing
// SerialInterface members. `ConsoleSessionObservedHistorically` is the operator's account of a session that
// happened (the mtjade1 attestation, srv13's captured SOL logs); it is admitted only from a read that exists in
// the source, it carries a transcription standing rather than an observation one, and pre-OS console evidence
// is not admissible as any of the three (v1: a post-hoc firmware boot-selection line is not a first-contact
// read, and here it is not admitted as a console reading either).
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type ConsoleRouteReading
  = ConsoleConfigured { transport: SolConsoleTransport, configuration: NonEmptyStr }
  | ConsoleServiceAdvertised { transport: SolConsoleTransport, advertisement: NonEmptyStr }
  | ConsoleSessionObservedHistorically { transport: SolConsoleTransport, evidence: NonEmptyStr }

// THE HISTORICAL EVIDENCE, AS SUPPLIED BY ITS OWNER. `from_read` is the read identity it came from; the fold
// admits it only if that read exists in the source for this controller, so an account cannot be attached to a
// board nobody read. The standing it carries is supplied by its owner and stays TranscribedUncited for an
// operator attestation — a person's account of a session is not an observation receipt.
type ConsoleSessionEvidenceKind
  = CapturedSessionLog
  | OperatorSessionAttestation

type ConsoleSessionEvidence {
  transport: SolConsoleTransport
  evidence: NonEmptyStr
  kind: ConsoleSessionEvidenceKind
  from_read: NonEmptyStr
  standing: CitedFigureStanding
}

fn bmc_console_reading(
  source: BmcReadSource,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
  standings: BmcReadingStandings,
  session: ConsoleSessionEvidence?,
) -> FirstContactReading<ConsoleRouteReading> uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match bmc_admitted_session(source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, session: session) {
    Present { value: admitted } => ReadingEstablished {
      value: ConsoleSessionObservedHistorically { transport: admitted.transport, evidence: admitted.evidence },
      standing: admitted.standing,
    }
    Absent => {
      let sol = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_ipmi_sol_info_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
      let advertised = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_redfish_serial_interfaces_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
      match bmc_console_advertised(body: advertised) {
        Present { value: advertisement } => ReadingEstablished {
          value: ConsoleServiceAdvertised { transport: RedfishSerialInterface, advertisement: advertisement },
          standing: bmc_body_standing(body: advertised),
        }
        Absent => match bmc_console_configured(body: sol) {
          Present { value: configuration } => ReadingEstablished {
            value: ConsoleConfigured { transport: IpmiSol, configuration: configuration },
            standing: bmc_body_standing(body: sol),
          }
          Absent => bmc_console_missing(sol: sol, advertised: advertised)
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// AN ACCOUNT IS ADMITTED ONLY AS ITSELF. A captured session log must name a read this source actually holds —
// otherwise the "log" is attached to a board nobody read. An operator attestation is admitted as an attestation
// (and keeps its transcription standing), which is the honest form of "the operator told us a session worked".
// What is NOT admitted, at all: a post-hoc pre-OS console line. There is no arm to pass one in, because a
// firmware boot-selection line on a console is not a first-contact reading of the controller (v1, and it stays
// true: the only way to say it is to say it from the BMC's own bytes).
fn bmc_source_holds_read(source: BmcReadSource, read: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  count(filter(bmc_source_read_names(source: source), name => name == read)) > 0
}

// A citation that NAMES a read (a GET, a POST, an ipmitool command, a Redfish path) is a claim about bytes: it
// is admitted only when this very source holds that read — otherwise a post-hoc pre-OS console line would be
// laundered into a first-contact console session. A plain-language citation ("the operator, via the intake
// record") is admitted as what it is: the operator's word, carrying `TranscribedUncited` and its read
// obligation, never the receipt a committed capture alone may mint.
fn bmc_session_citation_cites_a_read(citation: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  starts_with(s: citation as String, prefix: "GET ")
    || starts_with(s: citation as String, prefix: "POST ")
    || starts_with(s: citation as String, prefix: "ipmitool ")
    || string_contains(s: citation as String, pattern: "/redfish/")
}

fn bmc_on_the_operators_word(supplied: ConsoleSessionEvidence) -> ConsoleSessionEvidence {
  ConsoleSessionEvidence {
    transport: supplied.transport,
    evidence: supplied.evidence,
    kind: supplied.kind,
    from_read: supplied.from_read,
    standing: TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: "no capture backs this console session; it stands on the operator's account and owes the log that would ground it" as NonEmptyStr },
  }
}

fn bmc_admitted_session(source: BmcReadSource, controller: NonEmptyStr, observed_at: NonEmptyStr, session: ConsoleSessionEvidence?) -> ConsoleSessionEvidence? {
  match session {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: supplied } => match supplied.kind {
      CapturedSessionLog =>
        if bmc_source_holds_read(source: source, read: supplied.from_read) { Present { value: supplied } } else { none }
      OperatorSessionAttestation =>
        if bmc_session_citation_cites_a_read(citation: supplied.from_read) {
          if bmc_source_holds_read(source: source, read: supplied.from_read) { Present { value: bmc_on_the_operators_word(supplied: supplied) } } else { none }
        } else {
          Present { value: bmc_on_the_operators_word(supplied: supplied) }
        }
    }
  }
}

// WHAT THE SOURCE CAN ANSWER, read identities only — used to check an admitted historical claim really came from
// a read this source holds.
fn bmc_source_read_names(source: BmcReadSource) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match source {
    CommittedCaptureSet { controller: controller, manifest: manifest } => map(manifest, row => row.read)
    ModeledController { controller: controller, observed_at: epoch, realization: realization, responses: responses } => map(responses, response => response.read)
  }
}

fn bmc_console_configured(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match ipmi_sol_configuration_of_sol_info(stdout: text) {
        Present { value: configuration } =>
          Present { value: concat(
            "ipmitool sol info: Enabled=", bmc_yes_no(value: configuration.enabled),
            ", privilege ", configuration.privilege_level as String,
            ", payload channel ", configuration.payload_channel as String,
            " (a configuration read, not a host-payload capture)",
          ) as NonEmptyStr }
        Absent => none
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

fn bmc_console_advertised(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match redfish_member_ids(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: ids } =>
          if count(ids) == 0 {
            none
          } else {
            Present { value: concat("the Redfish service publishes ", int_to_decimal_string(value: count(ids)), " SerialInterface member(s)") as NonEmptyStr }
          }
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => none
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

// THE LAST CONSOLE ARM: nothing was advertised and nothing was configured. Three answers are kept apart here,
// and the middle one is what used to be lost. A read that was REFUSED (the fetch failed) refuses; a read whose
// body ARRIVED AND COULD NOT BE PARSED also refuses, naming what arrived -- under the previous arms that case
// fell through to the obligation arm and reported a debt for a read that had in fact been taken, which is the
// same collapse R5 was corrected for. Only a read that was never scheduled produces an obligation, and the
// obligation is never an empty string.
fn bmc_console_missing(sol: BmcBodyAttempt, advertised: BmcBodyAttempt) -> FirstContactReading<ConsoleRouteReading> {
  let refused = bmc_filter_some([
    bmc_body_refusal_note(body: sol, prefix: "SOL configuration"),
    bmc_body_refusal_note(body: advertised, prefix: "Redfish SerialInterfaces"),
    bmc_sol_info_unparsed_note(body: sol),
    bmc_serial_interfaces_unparsed_note(body: advertised),
  ])
  let owed = bmc_filter_some([bmc_body_owed_note(body: sol), bmc_body_owed_note(body: advertised)])
  if count(refused) > 0 {
    reading_refused(join(map(refused, cause => cause as String), "; ") as NonEmptyStr)
  } else {
    if count(owed) > 0 {
      reading_not_taken(join(map(owed, obligation => obligation as String), "; ") as NonEmptyStr)
    } else {
      reading_not_taken(bmc_console_route_obligation)
    }
  }
}

// THE SOL CONFIGURATION OUTPUT ARRIVED AND THIS READER CANNOT READ IT. `ipmitool_print_read`'s parser answers
// Absent both for output with no `Sol` section and for output that is not a print table at all; either way the
// read was taken and the answer is undecodable, so the reading says so instead of asking for the read again.
fn bmc_sol_info_unparsed_note(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match ipmi_sol_configuration_of_sol_info(stdout: text) {
        Present { value: configuration } => none
        Absent => Present { value: "the SOL configuration output arrived but names no Sol section, so this reader cannot tell whether a console route exists" as NonEmptyStr }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

// THE SERIAL-INTERFACES COLLECTION ARRIVED AND ITS MEMBER LIST DID NOT DECODE. Distinct from the fetch failing:
// the service answered and this reader could not understand the answer.
fn bmc_serial_interfaces_unparsed_note(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match redfish_member_ids(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: ids } => none
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => Present {
          value: concat("Redfish SerialInterfaces: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr,
        }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

// ── WHAT THE R5 READS COME BACK AS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Four sums, because each of R5's reads has four outcomes and the reading has to report the one that happened.
// The acquisition contract says attempted-plus-undecodable is a REFUSAL and never-scheduled is an OBLIGATION;
// a reading that turns a refusal into an empty list, or an obligation into an absence, breaks that contract at
// the grain of a single resource. These types keep the four apart end to end, and the driver below consults
// them in that order: a refusal first, then the service's own negative answer, then the inventory, and an
// obligation only when nothing was attempted.

// ONE ETHERNETINTERFACE MEMBER READ. A port with a published MAC is a candidate. A port the service published
// WITHOUT a MAC this reading can name is not an absent port: the read succeeded and the fact is unusable for a
// boot NIC, so it refuses with a cause that says which. A read attempted and refused (fetch or decode) refuses.
// A read never scheduled owes.
type BmcPortReading
  = PortCandidate { candidate: BootNicCandidate }
  | PortUnusable { read: NonEmptyStr, detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | PortRefused { read: NonEmptyStr, cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | PortOwed { read: NonEmptyStr, obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// THE BootOptions COLLECTION READ: listed; answered negatively by the service itself, which is an ESTABLISHED
// negative observation and not a failure (srv13's committed capture is that arm); refused; or owed.
type BmcBootOptionsAttempt
  = OptionsListed { options: List<RedfishBootOptionRead> }
  | OptionsAnsweredNotServed { message: NonEmptyStr }
  | OptionsRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | OptionsOwed { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// THE EthernetInterfaces COLLECTION READ: the count it announced, refused, or owed. The announced count is what
// makes a collection longer than the members read a refusal rather than a short list, so a refused collection
// read cannot be treated as "no announcement" and let a short inventory through.
type BmcCollectionAttempt
  = CollectionAnnounced { count: Int }
  | CollectionRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | CollectionOwed { obligation: NonEmptyStr }

// THE JOIN of an enabled BootOption to an inventoried candidate: exactly one pair (a selection), more than one
// (an ambiguity the reading refuses rather than resolves by taking the first), or none (no selection, which is
// not a failure and leaves the inventory to speak).
type BmcBootOptionJoin
  = JoinSelected { candidate: BootNicCandidate, boot_option: NonEmptyStr, device_path: NonEmptyStr }
  | JoinAmbiguous { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | JoinNone

fn bmc_port_readings(
  source: BmcReadSource,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
  standings: BmcReadingStandings,
  interface_reads: List<NonEmptyStr>,
) -> List<BmcPortReading> uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let empty: List<BmcPortReading> = []
  fold(interface_reads, init: empty, f: (acc, read) => concat(acc, [bmc_port_reading_of(
    source: source,
    controller: controller,
    observed_at: observed_at,
    standings: standings,
    read: read,
  )]))
}

fn bmc_port_reading_of(
  source: BmcReadSource,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
  standings: BmcReadingStandings,
  read: NonEmptyStr,
) -> BmcPortReading uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings) {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match redfish_ethernet_interface_of(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: interface } => match interface.mac_address {
          // The address is already parsed: `extdeps.bmc.redfish_network_boot` refuses the whole resource when
          // `MACAddress` is published but is not six hex octets (which covers the empty string and every other
          // malformed spelling), so a candidate here is a port the service really published an address for.
          Present { value: mac } => PortCandidate { candidate: BootNicCandidate {
            name: match interface.name {
              Present { value: name } => if name == "" { interface.id } else { name as NonEmptyStr }
              Absent => interface.id
            },
            mac_address: mac,
            source: read,
          } }
          // The property is absent: the port exists and the service chose not to publish its MAC. For a boot
          // NIC that is a refusal with a different cause from a malformed MAC -- the distinction the reader
          // above keeps, kept here too.
          Absent => PortUnusable {
            read: read,
            detail: "the EthernetInterface resource publishes no MACAddress, so no boot NIC can be named from it" as NonEmptyStr,
          }
        }
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => PortRefused { read: read, cause: cause }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => PortRefused { read: read, cause: cause }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => PortOwed { read: read, obligation: obligation }
  }
}

// THE CANDIDATES AMONG THE PORT READS. Refusals and unusable ports drop out HERE ONLY because the driver has
// already refused on them (`bmc_port_readings_refusal` runs first, and so does the collection check); no caller
// may use this list as a way to step past a failed read.
fn bmc_port_candidates(ports: List<BmcPortReading>) -> List<BootNicCandidate> {
  let empty: List<BootNicCandidate> = []
  fold(ports, init: empty, f: (acc, port) => match port {
    PortCandidate { candidate: candidate } => concat(acc, [candidate])
    _ => acc
  })
}

// THE FIRST WORD THE PORT READS GET. If any member read was attempted and refused, or decoded a port that
// cannot be named, the boot-NIC reading refuses and says which read and why -- before any inventory or
// selection is considered. This is the acquisition contract at this reading's grain.
fn bmc_port_readings_refusal(ports: List<BmcPortReading>) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  let details = map(filter(ports, port => match port {
    PortRefused { read: read, cause: cause } => true
    PortUnusable { read: read, detail: detail } => true
    _ => false
  }), port => match port {
    PortRefused { read: read, cause: cause } => concat(read as String, concat(": ", cause as String))
    PortUnusable { read: read, detail: detail } => concat(read as String, concat(": ", detail as String))
    _ => ""
  })
  if count(details) == 0 {
    none
  } else {
    Present { value: concat("an EthernetInterface member read: ", join(details, "; ") as NonEmptyStr) }
  }
}

fn bmc_boot_options_attempt(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> BmcBootOptionsAttempt {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match redfish_boot_options_of(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: BootOptionsListed { options: options } } => OptionsListed { options: options }
        ObservationEstablished { observed: BootOptionsNotServed { message: message } } => OptionsAnsweredNotServed { message: message }
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => OptionsRefused { cause: cause }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => OptionsRefused { cause: cause }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => OptionsOwed { obligation: obligation }
  }
}

fn bmc_collection_attempt(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> BmcCollectionAttempt {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } =>
      match redfish_ethernet_interface_collection_of(body: text) {
        ObservationEstablished { observed: collection } => match collection.announced_count {
          Present { value: announced } => CollectionAnnounced { count: announced }
          Absent => CollectionOwed { obligation: "the EthernetInterfaces collection answers without a Members@odata.count, so no port count is known" as NonEmptyStr }
        }
        ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => CollectionRefused { cause: cause }
      }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => CollectionRefused { cause: cause }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => CollectionOwed { obligation: obligation }
  }
}

fn bmc_selection_from(options: List<RedfishBootOptionRead>, candidates: List<BootNicCandidate>) -> BmcBootOptionJoin {
  let matches: List<BootOptionMatch> = []
  let joined = fold(options, init: matches, f: (acc, option) => concat(acc, bmc_option_matches(option: option, candidates: candidates)))
  if count(joined) == 1 {
    match joined.first() {
      Present { value: pair } => JoinSelected {
        candidate: pair.candidate,
        boot_option: pair.option.id,
        device_path: match pair.option.uefi_device_path { Present { value: path } => path Absent => "the enabled BootOption names no UEFI device path" as NonEmptyStr },
      }
      Absent => JoinNone
    }
  } else {
    if count(joined) > 1 {
      JoinAmbiguous { detail: concat("more than one enabled BootOption names an inventoried interface: ", bmc_join_pair_texts(pairs: joined)) }
    } else {
      JoinNone
    }
  }
}

fn bmc_join_pair_texts(pairs: List<BootOptionMatch>) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(map(pairs, pair => concat(
    pair.option.id as String,
    concat(" names ", concat(
      render_mac_address(addr: pair.candidate.mac_address) as String,
      concat(" (", concat(pair.candidate.name as String, ")")),
    )),
  )), "; ") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn bmc_boot_nic_reading(
  source: BmcReadSource,
  controller: NonEmptyStr,
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr,
  standings: BmcReadingStandings,
  interface_reads: List<NonEmptyStr>,
  observed_boot_use: HostBootNicEvidence?,
) -> FirstContactReading<BootNicReading> uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let ports = bmc_port_readings(source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings, interface_reads: interface_reads)
  let options_body = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_redfish_boot_options_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  let options = bmc_boot_options_attempt(body: options_body)
  let collection = bmc_collection_attempt(body: bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_redfish_ethernet_interfaces_collection_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings))
  let systems_body = bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: bmc_redfish_systems_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  match bmc_admitted_boot_use(source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, observed: observed_boot_use) {
    Present { value: historical } => ReadingEstablished {
      value: ObservedBootUseHistorical { candidate: historical.candidate, evidence: historical.evidence },
      standing: bmc_body_standing(body: bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: historical.from_read), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)),
    }
    Absent => match bmc_port_readings_refusal(ports: ports) {
      Present { value: cause } => reading_refused(cause)
      Absent => match options {
        OptionsRefused { cause: cause } => reading_refused(concat("the BootOptions read: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr)
        OptionsAnsweredNotServed { message: message } => bmc_boot_nic_without_selection(
          ports: ports, options: options, collection: collection, systems_body: systems_body,
          source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings,
        )
        OptionsOwed { obligation: obligation } => bmc_boot_nic_without_selection(
          ports: ports, options: options, collection: collection, systems_body: systems_body,
          source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings,
        )
        OptionsListed { options: entries } => match bmc_undecodable_option_paths(options: entries) {
          // A published `MAC(...)` node whose address does not parse is attempted-plus-undecodable, so the
          // reading refuses. Letting it fall through the join would report "no configured selection" -- an
          // absence -- for a question the controller answered in a form we could not understand.
          Present { value: detail } => reading_refused(concat(
            "a BootOption device path names a MAC(...) node this reader cannot decode: ", detail
          ) as NonEmptyStr)
          Absent => match bmc_collection_refusal(collection: collection) {
            // THE JOIN IS BUILT ON THE POPULATION THE COLLECTION AUTHORIZES. A configured selection names one of
            // the interfaces the collection says exists; if that collection read was attempted and refused, the
            // selection is a join over a population this battery does not know. The refusal surfaces here too,
            // before the join, the count and the obligations.
            Present { value: cause } => reading_refused(cause)
            Absent => match bmc_selection_from(options: bmc_enabled_options(options: entries), candidates: bmc_port_candidates(ports: ports)) {
              JoinSelected { candidate: candidate, boot_option: option, device_path: path } => ReadingEstablished {
                value: ConfiguredBootSelection { candidate: candidate, boot_option: option, device_path: path },
                standing: bmc_body_standing(body: options_body),
              }
              JoinAmbiguous { detail: detail } => reading_refused(detail)
              JoinNone => bmc_boot_nic_without_selection(
                ports: ports, options: options, collection: collection, systems_body: systems_body,
                source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings,
              )
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE COLLECTION'S OWN REFUSAL, IN ONE PLACE. Both places that may reason past it (the join in
// `bmc_boot_nic_reading`, the count and obligations in `bmc_boot_nic_without_selection`) ask this helper, so the
// refusal text has one authority and cannot drift between them.
fn bmc_collection_refusal_text(cause: NonEmptyStr) -> NonEmptyStr {
  concat("the EthernetInterfaces collection read: ", cause as String) as NonEmptyStr
}

fn bmc_collection_refusal(collection: BmcCollectionAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match collection {
    CollectionRefused { cause: cause } => Present { value: bmc_collection_refusal_text(cause: cause) }
    _ => none
  }
}

// THE ORDER IS THE MODEL. The driver has already surfaced the two refusals that outrank everything here — a
// member read that failed, a BootOptions read that failed — so what is left to decide is the collection, the
// count, the configured selection and the obligations, in that order.
//
// THE COLLECTION IS ASKED FIRST, AND ITS REFUSAL IS NEVER ANSWERED BY COUNTING. The collection is the authority
// over WHICH interfaces exist, so a collection read that was attempted and came back undecodable cannot be
// answered by the members that happened to be committed: the same malformed collection would refuse or turn
// into coverage debt depending on whether some unrelated member read produced a candidate. Candidate count
// cannot erase an already-observed collection failure, so the zero-candidate fallback sits BELOW this match,
// inside `bmc_boot_nic_from_candidates`, where it is reachable only when the collection did not refuse.
fn bmc_boot_nic_without_selection(ports: List<BmcPortReading>, options: BmcBootOptionsAttempt, collection: BmcCollectionAttempt, systems_body: BmcBodyAttempt, source: BmcReadSource, controller: NonEmptyStr, observed_at: NonEmptyStr, standings: BmcReadingStandings) -> FirstContactReading<BootNicReading> uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let candidates = bmc_port_candidates(ports: ports)
  match collection {
    CollectionRefused { cause: cause } => reading_refused(bmc_collection_refusal_text(cause: cause))
    CollectionAnnounced { count: announced } =>
      if announced > count(candidates) {
        reading_refused(concat(
          "the EthernetInterfaces collection announces ", int_to_decimal_string(value: announced),
          " port(s) and only ", int_to_decimal_string(value: count(candidates)),
          " member resource(s) with a published MAC have been read: ", bmc_candidate_text(candidates: candidates),
        ) as NonEmptyStr)
      } else {
        bmc_boot_nic_from_candidates(candidates: candidates, options: options, systems_body: systems_body, source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
      }
    // THE POPULATION WAS NEVER ANNOUNCED. The members that were read still stand on their own reads, and the
    // debt is carried into the standing note; with no candidate at all this is coverage debt, which is what
    // the not-taken arm inside `bmc_boot_nic_from_candidates` says.
    CollectionOwed { obligation: obligation } =>
      bmc_boot_nic_from_candidates(candidates: candidates, options: options, systems_body: systems_body, source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings)
  }
}

fn bmc_boot_nic_from_candidates(candidates: List<BootNicCandidate>, options: BmcBootOptionsAttempt, systems_body: BmcBodyAttempt, source: BmcReadSource, controller: NonEmptyStr, observed_at: NonEmptyStr, standings: BmcReadingStandings) -> FirstContactReading<BootNicReading> uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  if count(candidates) == 1 {
    match candidates.first() {
      Present { value: candidate } => ReadingEstablished {
        value: InventoriedCandidate { candidate: candidate },
        standing: bmc_candidate_standing(candidate: candidate, source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings),
      }
      Absent => reading_not_taken("no EthernetInterface resource has been read for this controller" as NonEmptyStr)
    }
  } else {
    if count(candidates) > 1 {
      reading_refused(concat(
        "the boot NIC is ambiguous: ", int_to_decimal_string(value: count(candidates)),
        " interfaces are inventoried and no enabled BootOption joins one of them: ",
        bmc_candidate_text(candidates: candidates),
      ) as NonEmptyStr)
    } else {
      // REACHED ONLY WHEN THE COLLECTION DID NOT REFUSE. `bmc_boot_nic_without_selection` asks the collection
      // before it counts, so an empty candidate list gets here only when the collection was announced
      // consistently or never announced at all — which is coverage debt, not a suppressed read failure.
      reading_not_taken(bmc_boot_nic_obligation(options: options, systems_body: systems_body))
    }
  }
}

fn bmc_boot_nic_obligation(options: BmcBootOptionsAttempt, systems_body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr {
  let no_nic = "no EthernetInterface resource with a published MAC has been read for this controller" as NonEmptyStr
  match bmc_boot_nic_override_note(systems_body: systems_body) {
    Present { value: note } => match options {
      OptionsOwed { obligation: obligation } => concat(obligation, note)
      _ => concat(no_nic, note)
    }
    Absent => match options {
      OptionsOwed { obligation: obligation } => obligation
      _ => no_nic
    }
  }
}

fn bmc_body_standing(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> CitedFigureStanding {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } => standing
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: "the read behind this reading was refused" as NonEmptyStr }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: obligation }
  }
}

fn bmc_body_refusal_note(body: BmcBodyAttempt, prefix: String) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } => none
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => Present { value: concat(prefix, ": ", cause) as NonEmptyStr }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

fn bmc_body_owed_note(body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } => none
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => none
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => Present { value: obligation }
  }
}

fn bmc_yes_no(value: Bool) -> String {
  if value { "yes" } else { "no" }
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// R5 — THE BOOT NIC, WHICH THE BMC CAN ONLY BE ASKED ABOUT.
//
// There is no BMC-side "boot NIC" read: the IPMI boot parameters carry no NIC field, so the question is
// answerable from the Redfish service — the EthernetInterface resources, and a BootOption whose UEFI device
// path names a MAC — joined to whatever the host layer has observed. Three kinds of answer, in increasing
// strength: a port the service INVENTORIES, a port the firmware is CONFIGURED to boot from (the join, and the
// only one that means what people assume it means), and a port whose boot use was OBSERVED, historically, from
// a BMC-side read.
//
// TWO RULES THIS SECTION EXISTS TO ENFORCE:
//   - NEVER THE FIRST INTERFACE. A collection with two members is not evidence that the first is the boot
//     port. If the join is ambiguous — two enabled boot options naming two different MACs, or no boot option at
//     all while more than one candidate is inventoried — the reading REFUSES and names what it could not
//     separate. The H11SSL capture set is exactly this case: its collection announces two members and only one
//     member resource is committed.
//   - A BOOT NIC IS A NETWORK FACT AND MINTS NO BMC CAPABILITY. Nothing here feeds R2, and the witness proves
//     it: a world with an established boot NIC and a PXE line reports no capability that mentions media or PXE.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A PORT, as read. `source` is the read identity that published it, so a candidate is always traceable to the
// member resource it came from.
// THE ADDRESS IS THE VALUE, NOT ITS SPELLING. It arrives already parsed by `extdeps.bmc.redfish_network_boot`
// from the wire text, and it is joined to a boot option by `extdeps.network.mac`'s own equality. A string here
// would put the case-and-delimiter question back where the answer is a substring test -- one that misses a real
// port published in the other spelling, and one that can match digits sitting outside any `MAC(...)` node.
type BootNicCandidate {
  name: NonEmptyStr
  mac_address: Eui48Address
  source: NonEmptyStr
}

// `BootNicRefused` is the arm the acquisition contract demands: a read that was ATTEMPTED and came back
// undecodable is a refusal -- never an empty inventory, and never the obligation that would have been owed had
// the read never been scheduled. The states the design separates are spelled once here: a property ABSENT (an
// absent optional, which the schema allows), a property MALFORMED (the reader's own refusal, naming the
// property), the service SAYING NOT SERVED (`OptionsAnsweredNotServed`, an established negative), NEVER READ
// (`PortOwed` / `OptionsOwed` / `CollectionOwed`), and a decoded inventory with no configured join
// (`InventoriedCandidate`, only when the inventory is unambiguous).
type BootNicReading
  = InventoriedCandidate { candidate: BootNicCandidate }
  | ConfiguredBootSelection { candidate: BootNicCandidate, boot_option: NonEmptyStr, device_path: NonEmptyStr }
  | ObservedBootUseHistorical { candidate: BootNicCandidate, evidence: NonEmptyStr }
  | BootNicRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// THE HOST-SIDE JOIN, HANDED IN. gunbc.host.host_network_diagnosis owns InterfaceObservation/BootNicAssignment
// and its `boot_nic_inventory`; a consumer that wants its rows reflected here projects them into this shape and
// passes them in, carrying its standing with them. The battery does not import the host layer: the boot NIC is
// a network fact whose authority stays where it was measured.
type HostBootNicEvidence {
  candidate: BootNicCandidate
  evidence: NonEmptyStr
  from_read: NonEmptyStr
}


// EVERY REQUESTED MEMBER RESOURCE, read and decoded. A member that publishes no MAC is not a candidate (a port
// that cannot be named cannot be joined), and a member whose read is owed is not silently skipped — the
// obligation is what makes the inventory honest.


// THE JOIN. Only an ENABLED boot option whose device path names a MAC counts, and the MAC must match exactly
// one inventoried candidate. Two enabled options naming different MACs, or one option naming a MAC nobody
// inventories, refuses with the candidates and the paths in the cause: the reader can see what did not join,
// which is the only way an operator can fix it.

// AN ENABLED OPTION WHOSE DEVICE PATH NAMES A MAC NODE. The node, not the substring: `MAC(` in the path text
// is what the filter used to ask, which let an option whose node holds garbage (`MAC(00ff,0x1)`) into the join
// and out of the other side as "no selection" -- a malformed answer reported as an absent one. Such an option
// is refused before the join, by `bmc_undecodable_option_paths`, so this filter only ever sees a node that
// parsed or a path that names no MAC at all.
fn bmc_enabled_options(options: List<RedfishBootOptionRead>) -> List<RedfishBootOptionRead> {
  filter(options, option => match option.enabled {
    Present { value: true } => match redfish_device_path_mac(path: bmc_option_path(option: option)) {
      PathNamesMac { address: address } => true
      _ => false
    }
    Present { value: false } => false
    Absent => false
  })
}

// THE PATH TEXT, or the empty string when the option published none. An empty path names no MAC node, so such
// an option leaves the join the same way it did when there was no path to compare.
fn bmc_option_path(option: RedfishBootOptionRead) -> String {
  match option.uefi_device_path {
    Present { value: path } => path
    Absent => ""
  }
}

// A BOOT OPTION WHOSE `MAC(...)` NODE THIS READER COULD NOT DECODE, named by its path text, or none when every
// enabled option's node parsed. The node was published and the address inside it is not an address: that is a
// read that was attempted and came back undecodable, so the reading refuses rather than reporting no selection.
fn bmc_undecodable_option_paths(options: List<RedfishBootOptionRead>) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  let broken = filter(options, option => match redfish_device_path_mac(path: bmc_option_path(option: option)) {
    PathMacUndecodable { text: text } => true
    _ => false
  })
  if count(broken) == 0 {
    none
  } else {
    Present {
      value: join(map(broken, option => concat(
        concat(option.id as String, " names device path "),
        bmc_option_path(option: option),
      )), "; ") as NonEmptyStr
    }
  }
}


type BootOptionMatch { option: RedfishBootOptionRead, candidate: BootNicCandidate }

fn bmc_option_matches(option: RedfishBootOptionRead, candidates: List<BootNicCandidate>) -> List<BootOptionMatch> {
  let matches: List<BootOptionMatch> = []
  fold(candidates, init: matches, f: (acc, candidate) =>
    if bmc_option_matches_candidate(option: option, candidate: candidate) {
      concat(acc, [BootOptionMatch { option: option, candidate: candidate }])
    } else {
      acc
    }
  )
}

// THE JOIN ITSELF, ON ADDRESSES. A DMTF UEFI device path spells a MAC as `MAC(ac1f6be3f6b0,0x0)` while Redfish
// publishes `AC:1F:6B:E3:F6:B0`; those are the same address in two upstreams' spellings, and joining them is
// what this reading exists to do. Both sides are PARSED -- the node's token by `extdeps.bmc.redfish_network_boot`
// (packed or colon form, either case) and the candidate's address at the extdeps boundary -- and compared by
// `extdeps.network.mac`'s equality. This is NOT version-string normalization: both spellings come from the same
// DMTF definitions, and nothing here compares a firmware version.
//
// WHAT THE OLD TEXT TEST GOT WRONG, and what the witness now pins: it asked whether the path contained `MAC(`
// anywhere and then whether the packed digits occurred anywhere, so (a) an uppercase Redfish MAC against EDK2's
// lowercase node answered "no boot option names this NIC" with confidence, and (b) hex digits belonging to a PCI
// function or an ACS parameter could satisfy the test for a port that the path never named.
fn bmc_option_matches_candidate(option: RedfishBootOptionRead, candidate: BootNicCandidate) -> Bool {
  match redfish_device_path_mac(path: bmc_option_path(option: option)) {
    PathNamesMac { address: address } => mac_addresses_are_equal(left: address, right: candidate.mac_address)
    _ => false
  }
}

// A DECLARED BOOT USE, ADMITTED OR NOT AT ALL. The historical arm exists for a host-side account of ONE
// board's boot, projected in by gunbc.host's consumer. It is admitted only when the read it cites is a read
// THIS source holds: a post-hoc pre-OS console line naming a boot device is not a first-contact read, and
// handing it to the battery does not make it one. A boot NIC is a network fact and mints no capability.
fn bmc_admitted_boot_use(source: BmcReadSource, controller: NonEmptyStr, observed_at: NonEmptyStr, observed: HostBootNicEvidence?) -> HostBootNicEvidence? {
  match observed {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: account } =>
      if bmc_source_holds_read(source: source, read: account.from_read) { Present { value: account } } else { none }
  }
}

// THE CANDIDATES, AS A REFUSAL NAMES THEM. An ambiguous boot NIC refuses by listing every candidate it could
// not choose between — the ambiguity is then on the record, in the form an operator can act on.
fn bmc_candidate_text(candidates: List<BootNicCandidate>) -> NonEmptyStr {
  let parts = map(candidates, candidate => concat(concat(candidate.name as String, " "), concat(
    render_mac_address(addr: candidate.mac_address) as String,
    concat(" (read: ", concat(candidate.source as String, ")")),
  )))
  if count(parts) == 0 {
    "no candidate was read" as NonEmptyStr
  } else {
    join(parts, "; ") as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// THE STANDING OF A CANDIDATE IS THE STANDING OF THE READ THAT TYPED IT. A candidate from a committed capture
// carries the receipt that capture earned; a candidate rendered by a simulated controller carries the
// transcription and its obligation. Nothing here upgrades one into the other.
fn bmc_candidate_standing(candidate: BootNicCandidate, source: BmcReadSource, controller: NonEmptyStr, observed_at: NonEmptyStr, standings: BmcReadingStandings) -> CitedFigureStanding uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  bmc_body_standing(body: bmc_body_of(source: source, request: bmc_request_for(read: candidate.source), controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, standings: standings))
}

// THE COVERAGE CHECK, BEFORE ANY SINGLE-CANDIDATE CLAIM. A collection that announces more ports than were read
// makes a one-candidate inventory claim false: the service said there are more, and one of them may be the boot
// port. The reading refuses naming both numbers, so the gap in the capture set is the thing on the record.

// NO CONFIGURED SELECTION: either one inventoried candidate (an inventory claim, which is what the service
// actually published), or a refusal naming every candidate so the ambiguity is on the record, or the coverage
// obligation when nothing was ever read.


// The obligation names what would close it, and — where the Systems resource WAS read — what the controller
// itself already said about boot selection. The Boot object's override state is reported as the controller's
// own fact; it is never promoted into a boot NIC, and an override target of "Pxe" stays a target, not a
// capability (DESIGN: PXE is not a BmcCapability).
fn bmc_boot_override_text(enabled: NonEmptyStr, target: NonEmptyStr) -> NonEmptyStr {
  let head = concat("; the controller's own Boot object reports BootSourceOverrideEnabled=" as NonEmptyStr, enabled)
  let middle = concat(head, concat(", BootSourceOverrideTarget=" as NonEmptyStr, target))
  concat(middle, " (a boot source, not a network interface)" as NonEmptyStr)
}

fn bmc_boot_nic_override_note(systems_body: BmcBodyAttempt) -> NonEmptyStr? {
  match systems_body {
    BodyAvailable { body: text, standing: standing, provenance: provenance } => match redfish_boot_override_of(body: text) {
      BootOverrideReported { enabled: enabled, target: target } => Present { value: bmc_boot_override_text(enabled: enabled, target: target) }
      BootOverrideAbsent { message: message } => Present { value: concat("; the Systems resource carries no boot override: " as NonEmptyStr, message) }
      BootOverrideUnreadable { cause: cause } => Present { value: concat("; the Systems Boot object could not be read: " as NonEmptyStr, cause) }
    }
    BodyRefused { cause: cause } => Present { value: concat("; the Systems resource could not be read: " as NonEmptyStr, cause) }
    BodyOwed { obligation: obligation } => none
  }
}

// The obligation names what would close it, and — where the Systems resource WAS read — what the controller
// itself already said about boot selection. The Boot object's override state is reported as the controller's
// own fact; it is never promoted into a boot NIC, and an override target stays a target, not a capability
// (DESIGN: PXE is not a BmcCapability).

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE CARRIER: what one first-contact battery run produces for one controller.
//
// Five readings, each in the three-arm carrier. `controller` and `observed_at` are carried on the reading
// itself, not only on its source: a reading that cannot say which board it is about and when it was taken is
// not attributable, and the fold would have refused to build it. `family` is the gate the readings were
// produced under — R1 is family-typed, so a run whose family was never observed says so here and R1 refuses.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
type BmcFirstContactReading {
  controller: NonEmptyStr
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr
  source: BmcReadSource
  family: BmcFirmwareFamilyStanding
  firmware: FirstContactReading<FirmwareIdentityReading>
  capabilities: CapabilityReading
  sensor_typing: FirstContactReading<List<SensorTypingObservation>>
  console: FirstContactReading<ConsoleRouteReading>
  boot_nic: FirstContactReading<BootNicReading>
}

// WHAT A RUN IS ASKED FOR. `source` is where bytes come from; `standings` is what each provenance is worth;
// `binding` and `interface_reads` and `console_session` are SUPPLIED facts with their own standing — the
// battery never invents a catalogue row, an interface, or a session. `observed_boot_use` is likewise only
// admitted as a historical account of one board's boot, never promoted into a first-contact read.
type BmcBatteryRequest {
  source: BmcReadSource
  controller: NonEmptyStr
  observed_at: NonEmptyStr
  family: BmcFirmwareFamilyStanding
  standings: BmcReadingStandings
  binding: BmcCapabilityBinding
  interface_reads: List<NonEmptyStr>
  console_session: ConsoleSessionEvidence?
  observed_boot_use: HostBootNicEvidence?
}

// THE BATTERY. One pass, five readings, no effect: every body arrives through `bmc_body_of`, which goes
// through `bmc_read_of`, which goes through the wire parsers in extdeps. Nothing here opens a socket, and
// nothing here writes a value back to a controller.
// R1 UNDER THE GATE. A release identity is family-typed, so an unobserved family refuses it by name rather
// than letting some source's version stand unattributed; the refusal says which read would settle the gate.
fn bmc_firmware_reading_under_gate(source: BmcReadSource, controller: NonEmptyStr, observed_at: NonEmptyStr, gate: BmcFirmwareFamilyStanding, standings: BmcReadingStandings) -> FirstContactReading<FirmwareIdentityReading> {
  match gate {
    FamilyObserved { family: family } =>
      bmc_firmware_identity_reading(source: source, controller: controller, observed_at: observed_at, family: family, standings: standings)
    FamilyUnobserved { discriminator: discriminator } =>
      reading_refused(concat("the firmware family is not observed for this controller (", concat(discriminator as String, "; a family-tagged release identity needs the Managers resource before any source can be agreed")) as NonEmptyStr)
  }
}

fn bmc_first_contact_reading_of(request: BmcBatteryRequest) -> BmcFirstContactReading uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let firmware = bmc_firmware_reading_under_gate(source: request.source, controller: request.controller, observed_at: request.observed_at, gate: request.family, standings: request.standings)
  let capabilities = bmc_capability_evidence_of(from_release: firmware, binding: request.binding, source: request.source, controller: request.controller, observed_at: request.observed_at, standings: request.standings)
  let sensor_typing = bmc_sensor_typing_reading(source: request.source, controller: request.controller, observed_at: request.observed_at, standings: request.standings)
  let console = bmc_console_reading(source: request.source, controller: request.controller, observed_at: request.observed_at, standings: request.standings, session: request.console_session)
  let boot_nic = bmc_boot_nic_reading(source: request.source, controller: request.controller, observed_at: request.observed_at, standings: request.standings, interface_reads: request.interface_reads, observed_boot_use: request.observed_boot_use)
  BmcFirstContactReading {
    controller: request.controller,
    observed_at: request.observed_at,
    source: request.source,
    family: request.family,
    firmware: firmware,
    capabilities: capabilities,
    sensor_typing: sensor_typing,
    console: console,
    boot_nic: boot_nic,
  }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// WHAT EACH PROVENANCE IS WORTH. Supplied once per run, read per body.
//
// The committed arm is the ONLY one that may carry an observation receipt, and only a zero-argument committed
// fold may mint it (`admit_callers` in extdeps.external_authority names those folds). A modeled response is a
// simulation and says so on every reading it produces; a runtime read has no producer yet in this change.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
fn bmc_modeled_standings() -> BmcReadingStandings {
  let simulated = TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: "a modeled response is a simulation of a controller, not a read of hardware; nothing about a real board follows from it" as NonEmptyStr }
  BmcReadingStandings { committed: simulated, modeled: simulated, runtime: simulated }
}

fn bmc_modeled_standing() -> CitedFigureStanding {
  TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: "this body was rendered by a BmcWorld, not answered by a controller; the read it would need is named by the obligation" as NonEmptyStr }
}

fn bmc_runtime_standing() -> CitedFigureStanding {
  TranscribedUncited { read_obligation: "the live reader has not run for this read; a runtime read carries its session receipt when one exists" as NonEmptyStr }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ACQUISITION: manifest row -> bytes -> record. One pass per row, fail closed.
//
// The order is the point: open the file, hash the bytes AS READ and compare to the pinned digest, unpack the
// intake bundle if the row names an observation key, split the capture framing (a transport marker is stripped
// and its status parsed, so a body never arrives with a status the parser has to guess), reconcile the status
// the capture declares against the status the bytes show, and only then build the record. Any step that fails
// produces a NAMED failure, and the manifest fold refuses the whole set — a half-read capture set is not a
// battery, and a silently missing row is the failure mode this root exists to close.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
type BmcAcquisitionFold
  = BmcRecordsRead { records: List<BmcReadRecord> }
  | BmcAcquisitionRefused { path: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

type BmcRowReading
  = RowRecorded { record: BmcReadRecord }
  | RowFailed { failure: BmcAcquisitionFailure }

type BmcAcquisitionFailure {
  path: NonEmptyStr
  cause: String
}

// THE MANIFEST FOLD. Reads each row exactly once, in order, and refuses the whole set on the first failure —
// so a fold can never report an established reading that rests on a capture it did not actually verify.
// The recursion is tail-recursive with the records threaded through: an effectful declaration that calls
// itself outside tail position has no realization (the compile route refuses it), and threading the
// accumulator keeps the order and the first-failure refusal exactly as they were.
fn bmc_read_manifest(manifest: List<BmcCaptureManifestRow>) -> BmcAcquisitionFold uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  bmc_fold_row_readings(rows: manifest, index: 0, read: [])
}

fn bmc_fold_row_readings(rows: List<BmcCaptureManifestRow>, index: Int, read: List<BmcReadRecord>) -> BmcAcquisitionFold uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  match list_at_optional(xs: rows, index: index) {
    Absent => BmcRecordsRead { records: read }
    Present { value: row } =>
      match bmc_read_row(row: row) {
        RowFailed { failure: failure } => BmcAcquisitionRefused { path: failure.path, cause: failure.cause }
        RowRecorded { record: record } => bmc_fold_row_readings(rows: rows, index: index + 1, read: list_snoc_item(xs: read, item: record))
      }
  }
}

// ONE ROW. Note what is NOT here: no defaulting, no "treat a missing file as an empty body", no permissive
// decode. Each of those would turn a coverage hole into a value.
fn bmc_read_row(row: BmcCaptureManifestRow) -> BmcRowReading uses fs: std.resources.Filesystem {
  let read = Filesystem.Read(path: row.path as String)
  match filesystem_read_outcome(content: read.content, success: read.success, error: read.error) {
    FilesystemReadRefused { error: error } => RowFailed { failure: BmcAcquisitionFailure { path: row.path, cause: concat("no file at the path this manifest row pins (", concat(error, "): the capture was never committed")) } }
    FilesystemReadSucceeded { content: contents } =>
      if sha256_hex_of_text(text: contents) == (row.digest as String) {
        match bmc_intake_observation(body: contents, key: row.intake_key) {
          ObservationRefused { cause: cause } => RowFailed { failure: BmcAcquisitionFailure { path: row.path, cause: concat("the capture bundle does not hold the observation this row names: ", cause as String) } }
          ObservationEstablished { observed: body } => bmc_framed_row(row: row, body: body)
        }
      } else {
        RowFailed { failure: BmcAcquisitionFailure { path: row.path, cause: "digest mismatch: the bytes on disk are not the bytes this row pinned, so the capture has moved under the manifest" } }
      }
  }
}

// THE FRAMING STEP, THEN THE RECORD. A declared status that contradicts the bytes is a refusal, not a
// preference — the capture either says what it got or it does not get believed.
fn bmc_framed_row(row: BmcCaptureManifestRow, body: String) -> BmcRowReading {
  let framing = bmc_probe_framing(text: body)
  match bmc_row_status_contradiction(row: row, framing: framing) {
    Present { value: contradiction } => RowFailed { failure: BmcAcquisitionFailure { path: row.path, cause: contradiction } }
    Absent => RowRecorded { record: bmc_record_of_framed_row(row: row, framing: framing, body: framing.body) }
  }
}

// THE INTAKE BUNDLE. Some capture sets ship one JSON file holding several command outputs under
// `observations`; a row that names a key is read as that observation and nothing else, and its digest still
// pins the WHOLE file — so one row cannot be swapped without moving the digest every sibling row checks too.
fn bmc_intake_observation(body: String, key: NonEmptyStr?) -> ObservationAttempt<String, NonEmptyStr> {
  match key {
    Absent => ObservationEstablished { observed: body }
    Present { value: wanted } => match parse_json_document(s: body) {
      JsonDocumentUnreadable { gap: gap } => ObservationRefused { cause: concat(concat(wanted as String, " (the bundle is not readable JSON: "), concat(json_document_gap_text(gap: gap), ")")) as NonEmptyStr }
      JsonDocumentParsed { value: document } => match json_field(obj: document, key: "observations") {
        FieldAbsent => ObservationRefused { cause: wanted }
        FieldMalformed { cause: _ } => ObservationRefused { cause: wanted }
        FieldRead { value: observations } => match json_field_string(obj: observations, key: wanted as String) {
          FieldAbsent => ObservationRefused { cause: wanted }
          FieldMalformed { cause: _ } => ObservationRefused { cause: wanted }
          FieldRead { value: text } => ObservationEstablished { observed: text }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn bmc_record_of_framed_row(row: BmcCaptureManifestRow, framing: BmcProbeFraming, body: String) -> BmcReadRecord {
  BmcReadRecord {
    controller: row.controller,
    read: row.read,
    observed_at: row.observed_at,
    operation: row.operation,
    outcome: bmc_row_outcome(row: row, framing: framing),
    digest: row.digest,
    body: body,
    provenance: CommittedCapture { manifest: row.path },
  }
}

// THE OBSERVED OUTCOME. A marker in the bytes outranks what the row declared; with no marker the declared
// outcome stands (that is what the capture script recorded). A row that declared an HTTP status the bytes do
// not show never gets here — `bmc_row_status_contradiction` refused it.
fn bmc_row_outcome(row: BmcCaptureManifestRow, framing: BmcProbeFraming) -> BmcReadOutcome {
  match framing.status {
    Present { value: code } => HttpStatusRecorded { code: code }
    Absent => row.outcome
  }
}

fn bmc_row_status_contradiction(row: BmcCaptureManifestRow, framing: BmcProbeFraming) -> String? {
  match framing.status {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: observed } => match row.outcome {
      HttpStatusRecorded { code: declared } =>
        if declared == observed {
          none
        } else {
          Present { value: concat(concat("the manifest row declares HTTP ", int_to_decimal_string(value: declared)), concat(" but the capture's own framing says ", concat(int_to_decimal_string(value: observed), "; a capture that disagrees with itself is not evidence"))) }
        }
      CommandExitRecorded { code: _ } => Present { value: "the manifest row declares a command exit for a capture whose bytes carry an HTTP status marker; the row and the bytes are from different reads" }
      OutcomeNotRecorded => none
    }
  }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// LOOKUPS. Records are asked for by read identity, or by the capture path they came from; the attribution
// gate (`bmc_record_is_for`) is applied by the readers that take a controller and an epoch, never skipped.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
fn bmc_record_for(records: List<BmcReadRecord>, read: NonEmptyStr) -> BmcReadRecord? {
  let matched = filter(records, record => record.read == read)
  if count(matched) == 0 { none } else { matched.first() }
}

// The path a committed record came from, when it came from a committed capture: the manifest names it, and a
// reader that wants "the file with this name in it" asks through here rather than inventing a path.
fn bmc_record_path(record: BmcReadRecord) -> String {
  match record.provenance {
    CommittedCapture { manifest: manifest } => manifest as String
    ModeledResponse { realization: realization } => concat("modeled:", realization as String)
    RuntimeRead { session: session } => concat("runtime:", session as String)
  }
}

fn bmc_record_by_suffix(records: List<BmcReadRecord>, suffix: String) -> BmcReadRecord? {
  let matched = filter(records, record => string_contains(s: bmc_record_path(record: record), pattern: suffix))
  if count(matched) == 0 { none } else { matched.first() }
}

fn bmc_body_by_suffix(records: List<BmcReadRecord>, suffix: String) -> String? {
  match bmc_record_by_suffix(records: records, suffix: suffix) {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: record } => Present { value: record.body }
  }
}

fn bmc_records_matching(records: List<BmcReadRecord>, infix: String) -> List<BmcReadRecord> {
  filter(records, record => string_contains(s: bmc_record_path(record: record), pattern: infix))
}

// THE REQUEST TEXT, AS THE UPSTREAM NAMES IT. A Redfish read is named by its method and URI, an IPMI read by
// the command line, a vendor read by method and target — the shape the upstream's own documentation uses, so a
// citation into this tree reads the same as a citation out of it.
fn bmc_request_text(operation: BmcReadOperation) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match operation {
    RedfishGet { uri: uri } => concat("GET ", uri as String) as NonEmptyStr
    IpmiToolCommand { command: command } => command
    VendorHttpRequest { method: method, target: target } => concat(method as String, " ", target as String) as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn bmc_record_request(record: BmcReadRecord) -> NonEmptyStr {
  bmc_request_text(operation: record.operation)
}

// The status a committed record carries, if its capture recorded one. `none` means "the capture did not say",
// never "it was fine" — consumers that branch on this must handle the silence.
fn bmc_record_http_status(record: BmcReadRecord) -> Int? {
  match record.outcome {
    HttpStatusRecorded { code: code } => Present { value: code }
    CommandExitRecorded { code: _ } => none
    OutcomeNotRecorded => none
  }
}

// THE FAMILY GATE, FROM THE CAPTURE SET ITSELF. No Managers resource in the set is not "some family we could
// not pin down" — it is an unobserved family, and the discriminator names the read that would settle it.
fn bmc_family_gate_of(records: List<BmcReadRecord>) -> BmcFirmwareFamilyStanding {
  match bmc_record_for(records: records, read: bmc_redfish_manager_read) {
    Absent => FamilyUnobserved { discriminator: "no Managers resource is in this capture set (GET /redfish/v1/Managers/<id>): the firmware family is unobserved until one is read" as NonEmptyStr }
    Present { value: record } => match manager_identity_observation_of(body: record.body) {
      ObservationRefused { cause: _ } => FamilyUnobserved { discriminator: "the Managers capture does not decode as a manager identity: the family gate needs a Managers resource that parses" as NonEmptyStr }
      ObservationEstablished { observed: observation } => family_standing_from_discrimination(discrimination: discriminate_family(observation: observation))
    }
  }
}

// The SOL session log read: a CAPTURED session log, not a configuration read, and the only console arm it can
// ground is the historical one. It is named here because a fold that claims a session must cite a read its own
// capture set holds.
data bmc_sol_session_log_read: NonEmptyStr = "captured SOL session log"

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE COMMITTED CAPTURE SETS. Each is one controller, one moment, one directory: the manifest pins the
// bytes and the fold is the only declaration allowed to mint an observation receipt for them
// (`admit_callers` in extdeps.external_authority names exactly these five). What a fold says is what its
// directory holds — a read the directory does not contain stays an obligation, and nothing here upgrades a
// bare, prose, or operator-attested row into an observation by consuming it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

